window.SEARCH_INDEX=[{"url":"admin.html","title":"Admin essentials","intro":"admin essentials the everyday tasks behind a well-run org: managing users, shaping the ui, moving data in and out, and keeping track of what changed.","sections":[{"id":"users","h":"Users","t":"creating a user setup → users → new user . username must be unique across all salesforce orgs and look like an email (it doesn't have to be a real mailbox): jane.doe@acme.com.uat is fine for a sandbox. choose the user licence and a profile (ideally a minimal one). set the role for record visibility. save, then assign permission set groups / permission sets and any permission set licences . bulk-create users with data loader (insert on user ) or the sf cli: sf org create user --definition-file config/user-def.json for scratch orgs. lifecycle action effect when freeze blocks login immediately; licence still used urgent offboarding while you reassign records deactivate frees the licence; user's records remain and stay owned by them normal offboarding delete not possible users can never be deleted, only deactivated reset password emails a reset link support requests login as log in as the user to see what they see debugging access (enable in login access policies) before deactivating , reassign open records (setup → mass transfer records for accounts/leads; data loader for others), check whether they're a default case owner, lead owner, workflow/flow email recipient, or the running user of a scheduled job or dashboard. deactivating the running user of a scheduled flow breaks it."},{"id":"apps-tabs-and-pages","h":"Apps, tabs and pages","t":"lightning app a named set of tabs (navigation items) with branding and utility bar. app manager → new lightning app. assign to profiles. tab every custom object needs a tab to appear in navigation. tab visibility (default on / default off / hidden) is set in profiles and permission sets. lightning app builder build record pages, app pages and home pages from components. activate a record page per org default, app, record type and profile. utility bar the footer of an app: notes, recent items, a softphone, a screen flow, a custom lwc. path a stage-by-stage guide on a record page with key fields and guidance for each picklist value. quick actions buttons that create related records, update fields, log calls or launch flows/lwcs. object-specific or global."},{"id":"list-views","h":"List views","t":"filter by owner ( my , all , my team's , a queue), field criteria with filter logic ( 1 and (2 or 3) ). share with all users, groups or roles. pin a default list view. display as table, kanban (drag records between picklist columns), or split view. inline-edit fields directly in the list view, and select several rows to edit a field on all of them at once."},{"id":"importing-and-exporting-data","h":"Importing and exporting data","t":"tool volume objects notes data import wizard up to 50,000 records accounts, contacts, leads, solutions, campaign members, person accounts, custom objects in setup. duplicate matching, can trigger workflow/automation or not. data loader up to millions all objects desktop app and cli. insert, update, upsert, delete, hard delete, export, export all (incl. deleted). bulk api option for large jobs. sf data commands any all scriptable; good for seeding sandboxes and ci. see devops . data export service whole org all setup → data export. weekly or monthly csv zip backup. reports → export report rows reportable objects quick extracts for users. a safe data load, step by step export first : back up the records you'll touch (data loader export with ids). prepare the file : one column per field, api names as headers, dates as yyyy-mm-dd , datetimes in iso 8601 utc, checkboxes as true/false , picklists using api values. use external ids to relate records without looking up salesforce ids: set account.erp_id__c on accounts, then load contacts with a column mapped to account:erp_id__c . think about automation : flows, triggers, validation rules and emails fire on loads. use a bypass custom permission for the loading user, or temporarily deactivate rules. load a small sample (10 rows) and check the results, success file and error file. tune batch size : lower it (e.g. 50) when triggers hit limits or you see unable_to_lock_row . verify with a report or a query count. firstname,lastname,email,account:erp_id__c,leadsource ada,lovelace,ada@example.com,erp-1001,web alan,turing,alan@example.com,erp-1002,partner referral"},{"id":"duplicate-management","h":"Duplicate management","t":"a matching rule defines how to compare records (exact or fuzzy on name, email, phone, address). a duplicate rule says what to do when a match is found: allow with alert , allow and report , or block , and for which users. standard rules for accounts, contacts and leads exist out of the box. duplicate rules run on ui and api saves; apex and data loader saves can be configured to respect them ( database.dmloptions.duplicateruleheader )."},{"id":"queues-and-assignment","h":"Queues and assignment","t":"a queue can own leads, cases, and custom objects (and some others). members work records from the queue's list view, taking ownership. lead and case assignment rules route new records to users or queues by criteria. only one rule per object is active; entries are evaluated in order. omni-channel pushes work to available agents by capacity and skills (see service )."},{"id":"email","h":"Email","t":"deliverability (setup → deliverability): sandboxes default to \"system email only\", so emails from flows don't go out until you change it to \"all email\". organization-wide email addresses : shared \"from\" addresses like support@acme.com , which must be verified. email templates : lightning email templates (with merge fields like {{{recipient.firstname}}} ) or classic (text, html, visualforce). email-to-case and enhanced email store emails as emailmessage records. set up dkim and spf so mail from salesforce isn't treated as spam."},{"id":"auditing-and-monitoring","h":"Auditing and monitoring","t":"tool shows retention setup audit trail every setup change and who made it 180 days in ui (export for longer) field history tracking old and new values of tracked fields 18 months (longer with field audit trail) login history logins, ip, browser, success/failure 6 months debug logs execution detail for a user or process trace flags last up to 24 hours; logs count against a 1,000 mb org limit apex jobs / scheduled jobs / paused flows async work and its status — event monitoring (shield) report exports, api calls, page views, logins event log files debug logs setup → debug logs → new trace flag for the user (or automated process, or a platform integration user). pick a debug level: for apex debugging set apex code = finest , database = info , workflow = info (flow also logs under workflow). reproduce the issue, then open the log. search for fatal_error , exception_thrown , validation_fail , flow_element_error , user_debug . the limit_usage_for_ns block at the end shows how close the transaction came to each limit. logs over 20 mb are truncated. lower the noisy categories (e.g. system , visualforce ) to none ."},{"id":"change-management-for-admins","h":"Change management for admins","t":"build in a sandbox, never directly in production (except for low-risk items like list views and reports). move changes with change sets (setup → outbound change sets → upload to production → deploy from inbound change sets) or devops center , which tracks work items and commits to git for you. write a short description on every field, flow and validation rule. the next admin will thank you. check where is this used? before deleting fields. deleted fields sit in the recycle bin for 15 days, then are gone along with their data."}]},{"url":"agentforce.html","title":"Agentforce & Data 360","intro":"agentforce & data 360 agentforce is salesforce's platform for ai agents that answer questions and take actions on your data. data 360 (formerly data cloud) unifies data from many systems so agents, analytics and marketing can use it. both build on the core platform you already know: flows, apex and permissions. this area changes fastest. product names, limits and setup screens shift every release. treat this page as a map of concepts and check current salesforce help for specifics.","sections":[{"id":"how-an-agent-works","h":"How an agent works","t":"flowchart lr u[\"user or customer (slack, lightning, web chat, api)\"] --> r[\"reasoning engine classifies the request into a topic\"] r --> t[\"topic instructions + allowed actions\"] t --> a1[\"action: flow\"] t --> a2[\"action: apex (invocable)\"] t --> a3[\"action: prompt template\"] t --> a4[\"standard actions (query records, summarise, knowledge)\"] a1 & a2 & a3 & a4 --> r r --> tl[\"einstein trust layer masking, grounding, toxicity, audit\"] --> u agent a configured assistant with a role, a channel (internal employee agent, customer-facing service agent, etc.) and a set of topics. it runs as an agent user whose permissions limit what it can see and do. topic a job the agent can do (\"check order status\", \"reschedule appointment\"), with a classification description, scope and natural-language instructions . action something the agent can call: an autolaunched flow, an invocable apex method, a prompt template, an api (via external services or mulesoft), or a standard action. the action's description tells the reasoning engine when to use it and what inputs mean. reasoning engine plans which topic and actions to use, asks for missing inputs, calls actions and composes the answer. agentforce builder setup → agentforce agents. configure topics and actions, test conversations, and see the reasoning plan for each turn."},{"id":"building-actions","h":"Building actions","t":"agent actions are mostly things you already know how to build. good descriptions matter as much as the code. public with sharing class orderstatusaction { public class request { @invocablevariable(label='order number' description='the customer order number, e.g. so-1001' required=true) public string ordernumber; } public class response { @invocablevariable(label='status' description='current fulfilment status of the order') public string status; @invocablevariable(label='expected delivery' description='estimated delivery date, if known') public date expecteddelivery; } @invocablemethod(label='get order status' description='looks up the fulfilment status and expected delivery date of one order by its order number.') public static list getstatus(list requests) { set numbers = new set (); for (request r : requests) numbers.add(r.ordernumber); map bynumber = new map (); for (order__c o : [select name, status__c, expected_delivery__c from order__c where name in :numbers with user_mode]) { bynumber.put(o.name, o); } list out = new list (); for (request r : requests) { response res = new response(); order__c o = bynumber.get(r.ordernumber); res.status = o == null ? 'not found' : o.status__c; res.expecteddelivery = o?.expected_delivery__c; out.add(res); } return out; } } least privilege : the agent user's permission sets decide what actions can touch. use with user_mode and user-mode dml in actions. keep actions narrow : \"get order status\" is better than \"run any query\". confirmations : mark actions that change data as requiring user confirmation. outputs can be shown to the user directly or used by the reasoning engine to compose a reply."},{"id":"prompt-templates","h":"Prompt templates","t":"prompt builder (setup → prompt builder) creates reusable prompts grounded in salesforce data. template types include sales email , field generation (fills a field on a record page), record summary and flex (any inputs). templates merge record fields, related records, flow outputs, apex outputs and data 360 data into the prompt. you are a support assistant for acme. summarise this case for a supervisor in 3 bullet points: the problem, what has been tried, and the next step. use a neutral tone. don't include personal data. case subject: {!$input:case.subject} description: {!$input:case.description} status: {!$input:case.status} call a prompt template from flow (as an action), from apex ( connectapi.einsteinllm.generatemessagesforprompttemplate ), from an lwc via apex, or as an agent action."},{"id":"einstein-trust-layer","h":"Einstein Trust Layer","t":"secure data retrieval and grounding respect the running user's permissions. data masking replaces pii before the prompt leaves salesforce, and restores it in the response. zero data retention agreements with model providers. toxicity scoring and audit trail (stored in data 360) for prompts and responses."},{"id":"testing-and-monitoring-agents","h":"Testing and monitoring agents","t":"use the builder's conversation preview to see which topic and actions were chosen and why. testing center runs batches of test utterances with expected topics, actions and responses — regression tests for agents. monitor production usage, escalations and feedback with agentforce analytics; review audit data for quality and safety. agents consume usage-based credits — know your org's pricing model before scaling up."},{"id":"data-360-data-cloud","h":"Data 360 (Data Cloud)","t":"data 360 ingests data from salesforce orgs and external systems, harmonises it into a common model, resolves identities into unified profiles, and makes the result available to segments, analytics, agents and apps. it can also zero-copy federate with warehouses like snowflake, databricks, bigquery and redshift instead of copying data. flowchart lr s1[crm orgs] --> ds[data streams] s2[\"web/mobile events\"] --> ds s3[\"warehouses (zero-copy)\"] --> ds s4[\"files / s3 / apis\"] --> ds ds --> dlo[\"data lake objects (raw)\"] dlo -->|mapping| dmo[\"data model objects (harmonised)\"] dmo --> ir[identity resolution] ir --> up[unified profiles] up --> ci[calculated insights] up --> seg[segments] seg --> act[\"activation (marketing, ads, orgs)\"] up --> ag[\"agents, prompt builder, data graphs, crm enrichment\"] data stream a connection that ingests data from a source on a schedule or in real time. data lake object (dlo) the raw ingested data. data model object (dmo) a harmonised entity in the customer 360 data model (individual, contact point email, sales order…). dlo fields are mapped to dmo fields. identity resolution match rules (exact email, fuzzy name + address…) and reconciliation rules that merge records from many sources into a unified individual . calculated insight a metric defined in sql over dmos, e.g. lifetime value or orders in the last 90 days. segment a filtered audience of unified profiles, e.g. \"customers with ltv > 5,000 who haven't ordered in 6 months\". activation publishing a segment to a target: marketing cloud, ad platforms, an org, a file. data graph a pre-joined, denormalised view of a profile and related data for fast lookups by agents and apps. data actions & triggered flows react to changes in data 360 by publishing events or starting flows in an org. from apex you can query data 360 with connectapi.cdpquery (sql); from the org ui, data 360 related lists and copy fields bring unified data onto crm records."}]},{"url":"apex.html","title":"Apex language","intro":"apex language apex is a strongly typed, object-oriented language with java-like syntax that runs on salesforce's servers. it has the database built in: queries and dml are part of the language. every line runs inside governor limits .","sections":[{"id":"where-apex-runs","h":"Where Apex runs","t":"entry point example triggers trigger accounttrigger on account (before insert) { … } lwc / aura controllers @auraenabled methods flow @invocablemethod actions async jobs queueable, batch, schedulable, @future web services @restresource classes, soap webservice methods anonymous apex developer console → debug → open execute anonymous, or sf apex run email services, visualforce controllers, post-install scripts messaging.inboundemailhandler , etc."},{"id":"types-and-variables","h":"Types and variables","t":"integer i = 42; // 32-bit long big = 9000000000l; // 64-bit decimal price = 19.99; // arbitrary precision — use for money double ratio = 0.333; // floating point boolean flag = true; // can also be null! string name = 'acme'; // single quotes only id accid = '001000000000001aaa'; date d = date.newinstance(2026, 11, 12); datetime dt = datetime.now(); time t = time.newinstance(9, 30, 0, 0); blob b = blob.valueof('hello'); object anything = 5; // any type; cast to use it // every uninitialised variable is null, including integer and boolean integer count; system.debug(count == null); // true // type conversion integer n = integer.valueof('12'); string s = string.valueof(3.14); decimal dd = decimal.valueof('10.50').setscale(2, roundingmode.half_up); null-safe operators string city = acc?.billingcity; // null if acc is null string owner = opp?.account?.owner?.name; string label = acc.name ?? 'unnamed'; // null-coalescing integer size = contacts?.size() ?? 0;"},{"id":"collections","h":"Collections","t":"// list: ordered, allows duplicates list colors = new list { 'red', 'green' }; colors.add('blue'); colors.addall(new list { 'cyan', 'red' }); string first = colors[0]; colors.sort(); boolean hasred = colors.contains('red'); // set: unique values, unordered set accountids = new set (); for (contact c : contacts) accountids.add(c.accountid); accountids.remove(null); // map: key → value map stock = new map { 'apples' => 10 }; stock.put('pears', 4); integer apples = stock.get('apples'); // null if missing if (!stock.containskey('plums')) stock.put('plums', 0); for (string fruit : stock.keyset()) system.debug(fruit + ': ' + stock.get(fruit)); // the most useful salesforce idiom: a map of records by id straight from a query map accounts = new map ( [select id, name, ownerid from account where id in :accountids]); // group records by a field map > contactsbyaccount = new map >(); for (contact c : contacts) { if (!contactsbyaccount.containskey(c.accountid)) { contactsbyaccount.put(c.accountid, new list ()); } contactsbyaccount.get(c.accountid).add(c); }"},{"id":"sobjects","h":"sObjects","t":"account acc = new account(name = 'acme', industry = 'technology'); acc.annualrevenue = 5000000; insert acc; // acc.id is now set // generic sobject access by field name sobject rec = acc; rec.put('rating', 'hot'); object v = rec.get('name'); string typename = rec.getsobjecttype().getdescribe().getname(); // 'account' // parent and child traversal from a query contact c = [select id, account.name, account.owner.email from contact limit 1]; system.debug(c.account.owner.email); account withkids = [select id, (select id, lastname from contacts) from account limit 1]; for (contact k : withkids.contacts) system.debug(k.lastname); // set a relationship by external id without querying contact newc = new contact(lastname = 'lovelace', account = new account(erp_id__c = 'erp-1001')); insert newc;"},{"id":"control-flow","h":"Control flow","t":"if (amount > 100000) { tier = 'large'; } else if (amount > 10000) { tier = 'medium'; } else { tier = 'small'; } for (integer i = 0; i 0) { queue.remove(0); } do { attempts++; } while (attempts"},{"id":"classes","h":"Classes","t":"public with sharing class discountservice { // constants and static members are shared within the transaction public static final decimal max_discount = 0.30; private static integer callcount = 0; // instance members private final map rates; public discountservice(map rates) { // constructor this.rates = rates; } public decimal discountfor(string tier) { callcount++; decimal r = rates.get(tier) ?? 0; return math.min(r, max_discount); } // property with a custom getter public integer rulecount { get { return rates.size(); } } // auto-property, read-only from outside public string lasterror { get; private set; } // inner class public class quote { public decimal listprice; public decimal net() { return listprice * (1 - max_discount); } } } access modifiers private (default), protected , public (within the namespace), global (across namespaces — needed for managed packages, rest resources, and some interfaces). sharing keywords with sharing , without sharing , inherited sharing . see security . always declare one. static statics live for one transaction only — there's no state between requests. use them for caches and recursion flags within a transaction. class size a single class can be up to 1 million characters; the whole org can hold 6 mb of apex (excluding tests and managed packages). interfaces, abstract classes, inheritance public interface notifier { void send(string recipient, string message); } public class emailnotifier implements notifier { public void send(string recipient, string message) { messaging.singleemailmessage m = new messaging.singleemailmessage(); m.settoaddresses(new list { recipient }); m.setsubject('notification'); m.setplaintextbody(message); messaging.sendemail(new list { m }); } } public abstract class shape { public abstract decimal area(); public virtual string describe() { return 'area: ' + area(); } } public class square extends shape { private decimal side; public square(decimal side) { this.side = side; } public override decimal area() { return side * side; } } // dynamic instantiation by name — handy with custom metadata notifier n = (notifier) type.forname('emailnotifier').newinstance(); enums public enum severity { low, medium, high } severity s = severity.high; system.debug(s.name() + ' ' + s.ordinal()); // high 2 severity parsed = severity.valueof('low');"},{"id":"exceptions","h":"Exceptions","t":"public class invoiceexception extends exception {} // name must end in exception try { insert invoices; } catch (dmlexception e) { for (integer i = 0; i you can't catch limit exceptions. system.limitexception ends the transaction. prevent them by bulkifying and checking limits methods."},{"id":"dml","h":"DML","t":"statement database method notes insert recs; database.insert(recs, allornone) sets ids on the records update recs; database.update(recs, allornone) only fields you set are changed upsert recs ext_id__c; database.upsert(recs, field, allornone) insert or update by id or an external id field delete recs; database.delete(recs, allornone) to the recycle bin; database.emptyrecyclebin() to purge undelete recs; database.undelete(recs, allornone) query deleted rows with all rows merge master dup; database.merge(master, dups) accounts, contacts, leads, cases; up to 3 at once // allornone = false: good records save, bad ones report errors list results = database.insert(contacts, false); for (integer i = 0; i"},{"id":"strings","h":"Strings","t":"string s = ' hello, world '; s.trim(); // 'hello, world' s.tolowercase(); s.touppercase(); s.contains('world'); // true s.containsignorecase('world'); s.startswith(' he'); s.endswith('d '); s.indexof('o'); // 6 s.substring(2, 7); // 'hello' s.substringafter(','); s.substringbefore(','); s.replace('world', 'apex'); s.split(','); // list string.join(new list { 'a', 'b' }, ', '); // 'a, b' string.isblank(s); string.isnotblank(s); // null-safe checks s.leftpad(20, '*'); s.abbreviate(8); string.format('{0} has {1} contacts', new list { 'acme', 3 }); 'it\\'s'.length(); // escape quotes with backslash encodingutil.urlencode('a b&c', 'utf-8'); pattern p = pattern.compile('inv-(\\\\d+)'); matcher m = p.matcher('ref inv-0042'); if (m.find()) system.debug(m.group(1)); // '0042'"},{"id":"dates-and-times","h":"Dates and times","t":"date today = date.today(); date nextweek = today.adddays(7); date eom = today.tostartofmonth().addmonths(1).adddays(-1); integer days = today.daysbetween(nextweek); // 7 integer months = today.monthsbetween(date.newinstance(2027, 6, 1)); boolean leap = date.isleapyear(2028); date weekstart = today.tostartofweek(); // locale-dependent (sunday in us) datetime now = datetime.now(); // stored and compared in utc string local = now.format('yyyy-mm-dd hh:mm', 'europe/london'); string iso = now.formatgmt('yyyy-mm-dd\\'t\\'hh:mm:ss\\'z\\''); date d = now.date(); // in the user's time zone date dgmt = now.dategmt(); // business hours businesshours bh = [select id from businesshours where isdefault = true]; datetime due = businesshours.add(bh.id, now, 8 * 60 * 60 * 1000l); // +8 working hours"},{"id":"json","h":"JSON","t":"// typed: define classes that mirror the json public class orderdto { public string ordernumber; public decimal total; public list lines; } public class linedto { public string sku; public integer qty; } orderdto o = (orderdto) json.deserialize(body, orderdto.class); string out = json.serialize(o); // or json.serializepretty(o) // untyped: maps and lists map m = (map ) json.deserializeuntyped(body); list lines = (list ) m.get('lines'); map firstline = (map ) lines[0]; integer qty = (integer) firstline.get('qty'); // reserved words: rename with a replace before parsing string safe = body.replace('\"currency\":', '\"currency_x\":'); // build json by hand jsongenerator g = json.creategenerator(false); g.writestartobject(); g.writestringfield('name', 'acme'); g.writenumberfield('employees', 250); g.writeendobject(); string json = g.getasstring();"},{"id":"useful-system-classes","h":"Useful system classes","t":"class use userinfo getuserid() , getprofileid() , getorganizationid() , gettimezone() , ismulticurrencyorganization() limits getqueries() / getlimitqueries() , getdmlstatements() , getcputime() , getheapsize() schema describe objects, fields, record types ( data model ) messaging send email ( singleemailmessage , templates with settemplateid and settargetobjectid ) crypto , encodingutil hashes, hmac signatures, aes, base64, url encoding http , httprequest , httpresponse callouts ( integration ) featuremanagement checkpermission('custom_permission') cache.org , cache.session platform cache get/put system debug , now , today , runas (tests), enqueuejob , schedule , url.getorgdomainurl()"},{"id":"anonymous-apex-for-quick-jobs","h":"Anonymous Apex for quick jobs","t":"// developer console → debug → open execute anonymous window list broken = [select id, email from contact where email like '% %' limit 200]; for (contact c : broken) c.email = c.email.deletewhitespace(); update broken; system.debug(broken.size() + ' contacts fixed'); anonymous apex runs as you, commits on success and counts against limits like any transaction. run it in a sandbox first."}]},{"url":"async-apex.html","title":"Asynchronous Apex","intro":"asynchronous apex async apex runs later, in its own transaction, with higher limits (200 soql queries, 60 s cpu, 12 mb heap). use it for callouts after dml, slow work you don't want users to wait for, and processing large volumes.","sections":[{"id":"comparison","h":"Comparison","t":"queueable @future batch schedulable start with system.enqueuejob(job) call the static method database.executebatch(job, size) system.schedule(name, cron, job) parameters any (object state) primitives and collections of primitives only object state object state returns a job id yes no yes yes (crontrigger) chaining enqueue one job from execute no start another batch from finish usually starts a batch callouts database.allowscallouts @future(callout=true) database.allowscallouts not directly — enqueue a job volume moderate small up to 50 million records via querylocator — per-transaction cap 50 enqueued per sync transaction; 1 from an async one 50 per transaction; none from batch/future 5 running at once, 100 waiting in the flex queue 100 scheduled jobs per org"},{"id":"queueable","h":"Queueable","t":"public class erpaccountsyncjob implements queueable, database.allowscallouts { private final list accountids; private static final integer chunk = 50; public erpaccountsyncjob(set ids) { this.accountids = new list (ids); } public void execute(queueablecontext ctx) { system.attachfinalizer(new syncfinalizer(accountids)); // runs even if this job fails list now = new list (); list later = new list (); for (integer i = 0; i accs = [select id, name, billingstreet, erp_id__c from account where id in :now]; for (account a : accs) { httprequest req = new httprequest(); req.setendpoint('callout:erp/customers/' + a.erp_id__c); req.setmethod('put'); req.setheader('content-type', 'application/json'); req.setbody(json.serialize(new map { 'name' => a.name, 'street' => a.billingstreet })); httpresponse res = new http().send(req); a.erp_sync_status__c = res.getstatuscode() (later))); // chain } } public class syncfinalizer implements finalizer { private final list ids; public syncfinalizer(list ids) { this.ids = ids; } public void execute(finalizercontext ctx) { if (ctx.getresult() == parentjobresult.unhandled_exception) { insert new integration_log__c(message__c = ctx.getexception().getmessage(), record_ids__c = string.join(ids, ',')); } } } delay : system.enqueuejob(job, 5) starts it after 5 minutes (0–10). check status : [select status, numberoferrors, extendedstatus from asyncapexjob where id = :jobid] . guard chaining in tests: only one chained job runs; check test.isrunningtest() or use asyncoptions to cap depth. prefer queueable over @future for anything new."},{"id":"future","h":"@future","t":"public class geoservice { @future(callout=true) public static void geocode(set accountids) { // primitives only — pass ids, not records list accs = [select id, billingcity from account where id in :accountids]; // ... call the geocoding api, set billinglatitude/longitude ... update accs; } } // geoservice.geocode(new map (trigger.new).keyset()); future methods can't be called from another future or batch context, can't be chained, and give no job id. they're also a classic way to avoid mixed_dml_operation (update a user in a future method)."},{"id":"batch-apex","h":"Batch Apex","t":"public class staleleadbatch implements database.batchable , database.stateful { private integer closed = 0; // kept across chunks thanks to stateful private list errors = new list (); public database.querylocator start(database.batchablecontext bc) { return database.getquerylocator([ select id, status from lead where isconverted = false and lastactivitydate scope) { for (lead l : scope) l.status = 'closed - not converted'; database.saveresult[] rs = database.update(scope, false); for (integer i = 0; i { 'crm-admins@example.com' }); m.setsubject('stale lead batch: ' + closed + ' closed, ' + errors.size() + ' errors'); m.setplaintextbody(string.join(errors, '\\n')); messaging.sendemail(new list { m }); } } // run with chunks of 200 (default) — or smaller if each record does heavy work id jobid = database.executebatch(new staleleadbatch(), 200); each execute call is its own transaction with fresh limits. a failure in one chunk doesn't roll back the others. scope size : 1–2,000 (default 200). use smaller scopes when each record makes callouts or heavy queries. iterable start : return an iterable<sobject> (or any type) instead of a querylocator for non-soql sources; limited to 50,000 items. callouts : implement database.allowscallouts ; up to 100 callouts per execute . abort : system.abortjob(jobid) or setup → apex jobs."},{"id":"schedulable","h":"Schedulable","t":"public class nightlyjobs implements schedulable { public void execute(schedulablecontext sc) { database.executebatch(new staleleadbatch(), 200); } } // every day at 02:00 (org time zone) string jobid = system.schedule('nightly stale leads', '0 0 2 * * ?', new nightlyjobs()); // or run once, n minutes from now system.schedulebatch(new staleleadbatch(), 'one-off stale leads', 10); cron expressions format: seconds minutes hours day_of_month month day_of_week [year] . use ? in one of day-of-month or day-of-week. expression runs 0 0 2 * * ? every day at 02:00 0 30 8 ? * mon-fri weekdays at 08:30 0 0 0 1 * ? midnight on the 1st of every month 0 0 18 l * ? 18:00 on the last day of every month 0 0 9 ? * 2#1 09:00 on the first monday of every month 0 0 * * * ? every hour, on the hour scheduled classes are locked. while a class is scheduled, you can't deploy changes to it (or classes it references) unless \"allow deployments of components when corresponding apex jobs are pending or in progress\" is enabled in deployment settings. keep the schedulable thin and put logic in other classes."},{"id":"monitoring","h":"Monitoring","t":"select id, apexclass.name, jobtype, status, jobitemsprocessed, totaljobitems, numberoferrors, extendedstatus, createddate, completeddate from asyncapexjob where createddate = last_n_days:1 order by createddate desc select id, cronjobdetail.name, cronexpression, state, nextfiretime, previousfiretime from crontrigger order by nextfiretime in setup: apex jobs , scheduled jobs , apex flex queue (reorder waiting batches). the org-wide daily async limit is the greater of 250,000 or 200 × user licences."},{"id":"other-async-options","h":"Other async options","t":"platform events published from apex and handled by a trigger or flow run in a separate transaction as the automated process user — a decoupled alternative to queueable ( events ). asynchronous paths in record-triggered flows. apex cursors ( database.getcursor ) let queueables page through very large query results across chained jobs."}]},{"url":"catalog.html","title":"Product catalog","intro":"product catalog \"salesforce\" usually means several products bought together. search or filter to find out what something is and when it's used. the badge shows how often you'll meet it. commerce cloud specialised b2c and b2b storefronts. use it for online stores and b2b reordering portals. clouds enterprise territory management specialised assign accounts to territories by rules and grant access accordingly. use it for sales teams organised by geography or segment. clouds experience cloud common websites and portals for customers and partners on top of your org's data (formerly communities). use it for customer self-service portals, partner portals. note external users get special licenses and external sharing. clouds field service specialised scheduling and dispatching mobile technicians: work orders, service appointments, mobile app. use it for installation and repair businesses. clouds industry clouds specialised pre-built data models and processes for financial services, health, manufacturing, public sector, nonprofit and more. use it for regulated or specialised industries. clouds knowledge common versioned help articles with data categories and channel visibility. use it for agent answers and customer self-service. clouds marketing cloud account engagement common b2b marketing automation (formerly pardot): forms, scoring, nurture, built closer to the core platform. use it for b2b lead generation and nurture. note prospects sync to leads/contacts. clouds marketing cloud engagement common email, sms, journeys and audience segmentation. a separate platform from core salesforce. use it for large-scale b2c marketing. note connected to core via marketing cloud connect. clouds omni-channel common service cloud routing engine that pushes work items to agents by capacity and skills. use it for contact centres handling cases, chats and messages. clouds revenue cloud / cpq common configure-price-quote, contracts, subscriptions and billing. use it for complex quoting with","sections":[]},{"url":"data-model.html","title":"Data model","intro":"data model objects are tables, fields are columns, records are rows. the differences from a relational database are in the relationship types, the built-in fields every object gets, and how the ui is generated from the schema.","sections":[{"id":"standard-crm-objects","h":"Standard CRM objects","t":"erdiagram account ||--o{ contact : \"has\" account ||--o{ opportunity : \"has\" account ||--o{ case : \"has\" account ||--o{ account : \"parent of\" contact ||--o{ case : \"reports\" opportunity ||--o{ opportunitylineitem : \"contains\" opportunity ||--o{ opportunitycontactrole : \"involves\" contact ||--o{ opportunitycontactrole : \"plays role\" product2 ||--o{ pricebookentry : \"priced in\" pricebook2 ||--o{ pricebookentry : \"lists\" pricebookentry ||--o{ opportunitylineitem : \"used by\" campaign ||--o{ campaignmember : \"has\" lead ||--o{ campaignmember : \"joins\" contact ||--o{ campaignmember : \"joins\" object api name purpose and key fields account account a company (or a person with person accounts). name , parentid (account hierarchy), industry , type , billingaddress , ownerid . contact contact a person. accountid (lookup, optional but usually set), email , reportstoid . with \"contacts to multiple accounts\", extra links live in accountcontactrelation . lead lead an unqualified prospect. status , company , isconverted , convertedaccountid / convertedcontactid / convertedopportunityid . opportunity opportunity a deal. stagename , closedate , amount , probability , forecastcategoryname , isclosed , iswon . case case a support issue. status , priority , origin , contactid , accountid , isclosed , ownerid (user or queue). task / event task , event activities. whoid points to a lead or contact; whatid points to almost anything else. both are polymorphic lookups. campaign campaign , campaignmember marketing efforts and who responded. products & price books product2 , pricebook2 , pricebookentry , opportunitylineitem catalogue, price lists, a product's price in a list, and a line on an opportunity. user user a login. profileid , userroleid , managerid , isactive . files contentdocument , contentversion , contentdocumentlink salesforce files: a document, its versions, and the links that share it to records."},{"id":"fields-every-record-has","h":"Fields every record has","t":"field meaning id 18-character unique id name the record's name: text, or an auto-number like inv-{0000} on custom objects ownerid user or queue that owns it (not on detail records in master-detail) createdbyid , createddate who created it and when (utc) lastmodifiedbyid , lastmodifieddate last change by a user or process systemmodstamp last change including system changes; indexed — use it for sync queries isdeleted true for records in the recycle bin (query with all rows ) recordtypeid present once the object has record types"},{"id":"field-types","h":"Field types","t":"type stores notes text / text area / long text area / rich text strings text up to 255; long text up to 131,072. long and rich text can't be used in soql where filters. number / currency / percent decimals precision and scale set at creation. currency respects multi-currency if enabled. checkbox boolean never null — defaults to false. date / date/time / time temporal values date/time is stored in utc and shown in the user's time zone. picklist one value from a list has an api value and a label (translatable). restricted picklists reject values not in the list, even from the api. multi-select picklist several values joined with ; awkward in reports and formulas ( includes() ). prefer a related object if you need to report on it. email / phone / url strings with format and ui behaviour click-to-email, click-to-dial, clickable links. lookup / master-detail / external lookup / hierarchy a reference to another record see relationships below. formula calculated at read time read-only; can span parent relationships. see formulas . roll-up summary count/sum/min/max of child records master-detail parents only. auto number sequential display value e.g. case-{00000} . not guaranteed gapless. geolocation latitude + longitude use distance() in soql and formulas. encrypted text masked text (classic encryption) limited; shield platform encryption is the modern option. field options worth knowing external id : marks a field as another system's key. it's indexed and enables upsert by that key from apex, the api, data loader and flow. unique : enforces uniqueness (optionally case-sensitive). gives an index too. required : at the field level it's enforced everywhere, including the api. requiring it on a page layout only affects that layout in the ui. default value : a formula evaluated when a new record is created in the ui or api (if the field isn't set). field history tracking : up to 20 fields per object; history is queryable in accounthistory , invoice__history etc. global value sets : one picklist value list shared by many fields. dependent picklists : values in one picklist controlled by another picklist or checkbox."},{"id":"relationships","h":"Relationships","t":"lookup master-detail required? optional by default (can be made required) always required delete parent clears the field, blocks the delete, or (for custom lookups) cascades — you choose deletes all children (cascade) ownership & sharing child has its own owner and sharing child has no owner; access is inherited from the master roll-up summary fields no (use flow/apex) yes reparenting always allowed only if \"allow reparenting\" is enabled limits up to 40 relationship fields per object (lookups and master-details combined) up to 2 master-detail fields per object; chains can be up to 3 levels deep standard object as child? yes no — standard objects can be masters but not details many-to-many with a junction object model \"a course has many students and a student takes many courses\" with an object in the middle that has two master-detail fields. erdiagram course__c ||--o{ enrollment__c : \"master\" student__c ||--o{ enrollment__c : \"master\" enrollment__c { string status__c date enrolled_on__c number grade__c } the first master-detail field you create is the primary master: it controls the junction record's look and feel and, together with the second, its sharing (users need access to both masters). roll-ups work on both parents. special relationship types self relationship : a lookup to the same object, e.g. account.parentid . hierarchical : a lookup from user to user ( managerid ). polymorphic : one field can point to several object types ( whoid , whatid , ownerid which can be a user or a queue). query with typeof (see soql ). external lookup / indirect lookup : links involving external objects ( __x ) from salesforce connect."},{"id":"relationship-names-in-code","h":"Relationship names in code","t":"each relationship has two names: the field (holds the id) and the relationship name (traverses to the related record). standard custom id field on child contact.accountid invoice_line__c.invoice__c child → parent traversal contact.account.name invoice_line__c.invoice__r.status__c parent → children (subquery) (select id from contacts) (select id from invoice_lines__r) the child relationship name ( invoice_lines ) is set on the lookup field when you create it. check it in object manager → the child object → the field → \"child relationship name\"."},{"id":"api-name-suffixes","h":"API name suffixes","t":"suffix meaning example __c custom object or field invoice__c , account.tier__c __r custom relationship traversal invoice__r.name __mdt custom metadata type tax_rate__mdt __e platform event order_shipped__e __x external object erp_order__x __b big object audit_event__b __share sharing table of a custom object invoice__share __history field history of a custom object invoice__history __changeevent change data capture event invoice__changeevent ns__ prefix managed-package namespace sbqq__quote__c"},{"id":"record-types","h":"Record types","t":"a record type lets one object support different processes. each record type has its own picklist values , page layout assignment (per profile) and, for leads, opportunities, cases and solutions, its own business process (which stages or statuses are available). example: opportunity record types new business and renewal , where renewal has fewer stages and a different layout. users need the record type assigned (via profile or permission set) to create records of that type. id renewalrt = schema.sobjecttype.opportunity .getrecordtypeinfosbydevelopername() .get('renewal') .getrecordtypeid(); insert new opportunity(name = 'acme renewal', recordtypeid = renewalrt, stagename = 'prospecting', closedate = date.today().addmonths(2)); don't use record types just to change the layout. if only the visible fields differ, use dynamic forms visibility rules on a single lightning record page. record types are for different processes."},{"id":"page-layouts-lightning-pages-and-dynamic-forms","h":"Page layouts, Lightning pages and Dynamic Forms","t":"page layout which fields, related lists, buttons and quick actions appear, and which fields are read-only or required in the ui. assigned by profile × record type. lightning record page the page's overall structure built in lightning app builder: tabs, components, related lists, custom lwcs. assigned by app, record type and profile (\"activation\"). dynamic forms place individual fields and field sections directly on the lightning page, with visibility rules (e.g. show loss reason only when stage = closed lost). replaces most uses of multiple page layouts. dynamic actions show buttons/actions conditionally (e.g. show \"escalate\" only for high-priority cases). compact layout the fields shown in the record highlights panel at the top and on mobile. search layout columns shown in search results and lookup dialogs."},{"id":"schema-tools","h":"Schema tools","t":"schema builder (setup → schema builder): a drag-and-drop erd of your objects; also creates objects and fields. object manager → fields & relationships → where is this used? : shows formulas, flows, apex and layouts that reference a field. check before deleting. describe calls in apex give you schema at runtime: schema.describesobjectresult d = account.sobjecttype.getdescribe(); system.debug(d.getlabel() + ' / ' + d.getlabelplural()); system.debug('can create? ' + d.iscreateable()); for (schema.sobjectfield f : d.fields.getmap().values()) { schema.describefieldresult fd = f.getdescribe(); if (fd.iscustom()) system.debug(fd.getname() + ' : ' + fd.gettype()); } // picklist values for (schema.picklistentry e : account.industry.getdescribe().getpicklistvalues()) { if (e.isactive()) system.debug(e.getvalue() + ' = ' + e.getlabel()); } // object by name (dynamic) schema.sobjecttype t = schema.getglobaldescribe().get('invoice__c'); sobject rec = t.newsobject(); rec.put('name', 'inv-1');"},{"id":"other-data-containers","h":"Other data containers","t":"container use for notes custom metadata types ( __mdt ) configuration that deploys with code: mappings, thresholds, feature flags read with my_setting__mdt.getinstance('devname') or getall() — no soql limit used. custom settings per-user/profile switches (hierarchy settings), cached config values are data, so they don't deploy. my_setting__c.getinstance() returns the most specific value for the running user. big objects ( __b ) billions of rows of history/audit data query only by the defined index; no triggers, no standard ui. external objects ( __x ) data that stays in another system salesforce connect queries the source live. platform cache fast, temporary key-value data org and session partitions; values can be evicted at any time."}]},{"url":"decisions.html","title":"Which one do I pick?","intro":"which one do i pick? the design questions that come up every week, answered as tables. each links to the chapter with the details.","sections":[{"id":"flow-or-apex","h":"Flow or Apex?","t":"situation pick why set fields on the record being saved before-save flow fast, no dml, admin-maintainable create/update related records with straightforward logic after-save flow visible to admins; bulkified automatically guided ui for users screen flow (embed lwcs where needed) fast to build and change complex logic, many branches, reusable services apex easier to test, review, refactor and version processing hundreds of thousands of records batch apex chunked with fresh limits per chunk callouts with complex request/response handling apex (queueable) full control over http, json, retries validation that needs other records apex trigger or flow with custom error validation rules can't query flow needs one thing it can't do flow + invocable apex action keep the process in flow, the hard part in code the object already has heavy apex triggers apex one tool per object keeps the order of execution understandable details: flow , triggers ."},{"id":"lookup-or-master-detail","h":"Lookup or master-detail?","t":"need pick child can't exist without the parent; delete together master-detail roll-up summary fields master-detail (or a roll-up via flow/apex on a lookup) child needs its own owner and sharing lookup relationship is optional lookup child is a standard object lookup (standard objects can't be details) many-to-many junction object with two master-details parent has huge numbers of children with frequent updates consider lookup — master-detail locks the parent during child saves and roll-up recalculation details: data model ."},{"id":"how-do-i-roll-up-child-data","h":"How do I roll up child data?","t":"option works on notes roll-up summary field master-detail real-time, no code, count/sum/min/max with filters record-triggered flow lookups needs flows on create, update, delete of the child apex trigger with an aggregate query lookups most control; handle re-parenting and deletes ( example ) scheduled batch any when \"nightly\" is fresh enough and volumes are large report or dashboard any when you only need to see it, not store it"},{"id":"where-do-i-store-configuration","h":"Where do I store configuration?","t":"need pick notes settings that deploy with code custom metadata type readable without soql limits; editable in setup per-user or per-profile switches hierarchy custom setting values are data — not deployed feature access for some users custom permission assign via permission set; check in formulas, flow, apex, lwc endpoints, secrets, tokens named + external credential never in code or custom metadata user-facing text, translatable custom labels system.label.x , $label.x , @salesforce/label/c.x large reference tables users maintain custom object reportable, with sharing and history"},{"id":"which-async-apex","h":"Which async Apex?","t":"need pick run after the transaction, with complex parameters queueable callout from a trigger queueable with database.allowscallouts retry later with a delay queueable enqueued with a delay process millions of records batch apex run on a schedule schedulable (usually starts a batch) or a schedule-triggered flow decouple publisher from subscriber; many listeners platform event update a user alongside other records (mixed dml) queueable or @future details: async apex ."},{"id":"which-data-tool","h":"Which data tool?","t":"records pick under 50,000; accounts, contacts, leads, custom objects; admins data import wizard any object, any volume, delete/upsert/export data loader scripted, repeatable, in ci or sandbox seeding sf data commands ongoing system-to-system sync an integration (bulk api, cdc, mulesoft) — not a manual tool regular full backups data export service or salesforce backup / a third-party backup tool"},{"id":"which-integration-pattern","h":"Which integration pattern?","t":"need pick external app reads/writes a few records rest api (composite for several) nightly load of 2 million rows bulk api 2.0 salesforce needs live data from erp when a user opens a record apex callout (via lwc) or salesforce connect warehouse should mirror salesforce changes change data capture + pub/sub api tell several systems an order shipped platform event custom business endpoint apex rest admins should call an api from flow external services or http callout action details: apis & callouts , events ."},{"id":"which-ui-option","h":"Which UI option?","t":"need pick show/hide fields based on values dynamic forms visibility rules guided multi-step process screen flow custom interactive component lwc a simple record form lightning-record-form in an lwc, or a quick action render a pdf visualforce renderas=\"pdf\" (or a document-generation app) customer or partner portal experience cloud with lwcs"},{"id":"how-do-i-grant-access","h":"How do I grant access?","t":"granting use object, field, app, tab, apex class access permission set (bundled in a permission set group) login hours, ip ranges, page layout assignment profile a persona (\"support agent\") permission set group, with muting if needed temporary access permission set assignment with an expiration date records owned by another team sharing rule (owner- or criteria-based) one record for one person manual share or a team access computed from a field (e.g. approver) apex managed sharing hide records despite sharing restriction rule details: security & sharing ."}]},{"url":"devops.html","title":"DevOps & the sf CLI","intro":"devops & the sf cli modern salesforce development keeps metadata in git, builds in sandboxes or scratch orgs, and deploys with the sf cli from a pipeline.","sections":[{"id":"release-approaches","h":"Release approaches","t":"approach what it is good for drawbacks change sets point-and-click bundles between orgs connected by deployment connections small admin teams no version control, no deletes, manual, slow devops center salesforce's ui that tracks changes as work items and commits to github/bitbucket admin-heavy teams adopting git less flexible than a full pipeline sf cli + git + ci (\"org-based\") metadata in git; deploy directories or manifests to each org most development teams you maintain the pipeline unlocked packages (\"package-based\") versioned, installable modules of your metadata with declared dependencies large orgs with clear module boundaries up-front work to untangle metadata third-party tools gearset, copado, flosum, autorabit, blue canvas… teams wanting comparison, rollback and compliance features licence cost"},{"id":"environment-strategy","h":"Environment strategy","t":"flowchart lr d1[\"dev sandbox / scratch org (per developer)\"] -->|pr| g[(main branch)] d2[\"dev sandbox (admin)\"] -->|pr| g g -->|ci: validate + tests| int[\"integration / qa sandbox (developer pro or partial)\"] int --> uat[\"uat / staging (full sandbox)\"] uat -->|release| prod[(production)] prod -. \"hotfix branch\" .-> hf[hotfix sandbox] hf -.-> prod each developer works in their own sandbox or scratch org. pull requests trigger a validation deploy (check-only, with tests) against an integration org. merges deploy to qa, then uat; releases go to production after sign-off. keep sandboxes in sync by back-promoting production changes and refreshing regularly."},{"id":"project-files","h":"Project files","t":"my-project/ ├── sfdx-project.json ├── .forceignore ├── config/project-scratch-def.json ├── manifest/package.xml ├── scripts/apex/ scripts/soql/ ├── data/ # seed data (sf data tree) └── force-app/main/default/ ├── applications/ classes/ triggers/ lwc/ aura/ ├── objects/invoice__c/ │ ├── invoice__c.object-meta.xml │ ├── fields/amount__c.field-meta.xml │ ├── listviews/ recordtypes/ validationrules/ ├── flows/ layouts/ flexipages/ ├── permissionsets/ permissionsetgroups/ ├── custommetadata/ labels/ staticresources/ └── namedcredentials/ externalcredentials/ { \"packagedirectories\": [ { \"path\": \"force-app\", \"default\": true } ], \"name\": \"my-project\", \"namespace\": \"\", \"sfdcloginurl\": \"https://login.salesforce.com\", \"sourceapiversion\": \"64.0\" } { \"orgname\": \"acme dev\", \"edition\": \"developer\", \"features\": [\"enablesetpasswordinapi\", \"communities\"], \"settings\": { \"lightningexperiencesettings\": { \"enables1desktopenabled\": true }, \"securitysettings\": { \"passwordpolicies\": { \"enablesetpasswordinapi\": true } }, \"chattersettings\": { \"enablechatter\": true } } } opportunitytriggerhandler opportunitytriggerhandlertest apexclass opportunitytrigger apextrigger invoice__c customobject * lightningcomponentbundle sales_rep permissionset 64.0 **/jsconfig.json **/.eslintrc.json **/__tests__/** # profiles are huge and org-specific; many teams don't deploy them **/profiles/** # settings you never want to overwrite in another org **/settings/security.settings-meta.xml"},{"id":"sf-cli-reference","h":"sf CLI reference","t":"install with npm install --global @salesforce/cli (or the installers), then check sf --version and update with sf update . every command has --help ; most take --target-org (alias -o ) and --json . orgs sf org login web --alias dev # browser login (production / dev org) sf org login web --alias uat --instance-url https://test.salesforce.com # sandbox sf org login web --alias hub --set-default-dev-hub # dev hub for scratch orgs sf org list # all authorised orgs sf org display --target-org uat # details (add --verbose for the auth url) sf org open --target-org uat --path lightning/setup/setuponehome/home sf config set target-org=dev # default org for this project sf org logout --target-org old-sandbox # scratch orgs sf org create scratch --definition-file config/project-scratch-def.json --alias feature-x --duration-days 7 --set-default sf org generate password --target-org feature-x sf org delete scratch --target-org feature-x --no-prompt # sandboxes (from production) sf org create sandbox --name dev2 --license-type developer --target-org prod --alias dev2 --wait 30 sf org refresh sandbox --name dev2 --target-org prod # users and permissions sf org assign permset --name sales_rep --target-org dev sf org create user --definition-file config/user-def.json --target-org feature-x scaffolding sf project generate --name my-project --template standard sf apex generate class --name invoiceservice --output-dir force-app/main/default/classes sf apex generate trigger --name invoicetrigger --sobject invoice__c --event \"before insert,after update\" --output-dir force-app/main/default/triggers sf lightning generate component --name invoicelist --type lwc --output-dir force-app/main/default/lwc sf project generate manifest --source-dir force-app --name package --output-dir manifest retrieve and deploy # retrieve sf project retrieve start --metadata apexclass:invoiceservice sf project retrieve start --metadata \"customobject:invoice__c\" \"flow:invoice_approval\" sf project retrieve start --manifest manifest/package.xml sf project retrieve start --source-dir force-app/main/default/lwc sf project retrieve preview # scratch/source-tracked orgs: what changed remotely sf org list metadata --metadata-type customobject # what exists in the org # deploy sf project deploy start --source-dir force-app sf project deploy start --metadata apexclass:invoiceservice apextrigger:invoicetrigger sf project deploy start --manifest manifest/package.xml --test-level runlocaltests # validate without saving, then quick-deploy the validated job sf project deploy validate --manifest manifest/package.xml --test-level runlocaltests --target-org prod sf project deploy quick --job-id 0afxxxxxxxxxxxx --target-org prod sf project deploy report --job-id 0afxxxxxxxxxxxx # status of a running deploy sf project deploy cancel --job-id 0afxxxxxxxxxxxx apex, data and logs sf apex run --file scripts/apex/fix-data.apex # anonymous apex sf apex run test --test-level runlocaltests --code-coverage --result-format human --wait 30 sf apex tail log --color # stream debug logs sf apex list log && sf apex get log --number 1 sf data query --query \"select id, name from account limit 10\" sf data query --query \"select id from apexclass\" --use-tooling-api sf data create record --sobject account --values \"name='acme' industry=energy\" sf data update record --sobject account --record-id 001xxxxxxxxxxxx --values \"rating=hot\" sf data upsert bulk --sobject contact --file contacts.csv --external-id email__c --wait 10 sf data export bulk --query \"select id, name from account\" --output-file accounts.csv --wait 10 sf data delete bulk --sobject lead --file lead-ids.csv --wait 10 # seed data with relationships sf data export tree --query \"select name, (select lastname, email from contacts) from account limit 20\" --plan --output-dir data sf data import tree --plan data/account-contact-plan.json --target-org feature-x"},{"id":"test-levels","h":"Test levels","t":"level runs when notestrun nothing sandboxes; production only when deploying no apex runspecifiedtests tests you list with --tests faster production deploys; each deployed class/trigger must reach 75% from those tests runlocaltests all tests not in managed packages default for production when apex is included runalltestsinorg everything, including managed packages rarely"},{"id":"deleting-metadata","h":"Deleting metadata","t":"deploying source never deletes anything by itself. use a destructive changes manifest: oldinvoicehelper apexclass invoice__c.legacy_code__c customfield # delete after deploying the rest (so references are removed first) sf project deploy start --manifest manifest/package.xml \\ --post-destructive-changes destructivechanges/destructivechanges.xml --test-level runlocaltests # or delete local source and the org copy together (non-production) sf project delete source --metadata apexclass:oldinvoicehelper"},{"id":"a-github-actions-pipeline","h":"A GitHub Actions pipeline","t":"name: salesforce ci on: pull_request: branches: [main] push: branches: [main] jobs: validate: if: github.event_name == 'pull_request' runs-on: ubuntu-latest steps: - uses: actions/checkout@v4 with: { fetch-depth: 0 } - uses: actions/setup-node@v4 with: { node-version: 20 } - run: npm install --global @salesforce/cli - name: authenticate (jwt) run: | echo \"${{ secrets.sf_jwt_key }}\" > server.key sf org login jwt --client-id ${{ secrets.sf_client_id }} --jwt-key-file server.key \\ --username ${{ secrets.sf_qa_username }} --instance-url https://test.salesforce.com --alias qa - name: lint lwc and run jest run: npm ci && npm run lint && npm run test:unit - name: validate deploy with tests run: sf project deploy validate --source-dir force-app --target-org qa --test-level runlocaltests --wait 60 deploy-qa: if: github.event_name == 'push' runs-on: ubuntu-latest steps: - uses: actions/checkout@v4 - run: npm install --global @salesforce/cli - run: | echo \"${{ secrets.sf_jwt_key }}\" > server.key sf org login jwt --client-id ${{ secrets.sf_client_id }} --jwt-key-file server.key \\ --username ${{ secrets.sf_qa_username }} --instance-url https://test.salesforce.com --alias qa sf project deploy start --source-dir force-app --target-org qa --test-level runlocaltests --wait 60 for large repos, deploy only what changed with the community sfdx-git-delta plugin ( sf sgd source delta --from origin/main --to head --output-dir delta ), which generates package.xml and destructivechanges.xml from the git diff. add static analysis with salesforce code analyzer ( sf code-analyzer run ), which wraps pmd, eslint and others."},{"id":"unlocked-packages","h":"Unlocked packages","t":"# one-time: register the package against the dev hub (adds it to sfdx-project.json) sf package create --name invoicing --package-type unlocked --path force-app --target-dev-hub hub # build a version (runs tests, checks 75% coverage with --code-coverage) sf package version create --package invoicing --installation-key-bypass --code-coverage --wait 30 # install in a sandbox, then promote and install in production sf package install --package invoicing@1.2.0-1 --target-org uat --wait 20 sf package version promote --package invoicing@1.2.0-1 sf package install --package invoicing@1.2.0-1 --target-org prod --wait 20 dependencies between packages are declared in sfdx-project.json . removing a component from a new package version deletes it (or deprecates it) on upgrade."},{"id":"things-that-don-t-deploy-cleanly","h":"Things that don't deploy cleanly","t":"data : records, custom setting values, users. seed them separately. profiles : retrieving a profile only includes permissions for the other components in the same retrieve. prefer permission sets. some settings (e.g. email deliverability, certain org preferences) must be set manually or with post-deploy scripts. active flows in production deploy inactive unless enabled in settings (see flow ). field type changes and deletions on fields used by apex or flows fail until references are removed. picklist values deploy but don't remove values from existing records."}]},{"url":"errors.html","title":"Errors & gotchas","intro":"errors & gotchas the errors you'll see most, what's actually happening, and the fix.","sections":[{"id":"apex-runtime-errors","h":"Apex runtime errors","t":"error cause fix system.limitexception: too many soql queries: 101 a query in a loop, or several flows/triggers adding up, or recursion bulkify ( patterns ); check the debug log's query count by line; add recursion control too many dml statements: 151 dml in a loop collect records in a list, one dml after the loop apex cpu time limit exceeded too much processing: nested loops, heavy automation on one object use maps, exit early, consolidate flows, move work async; profile with apex log analyzer too many query rows: 50001 query returns more than 50,000 rows in one transaction filter more; use batch apex or aggregate queries apex heap size too large holding too much in memory soql for-loops, query fewer fields, process in batches system.queryexception: list has no rows for assignment to sobject single-record assignment from a query that returned nothing query into a list and check isempty() list has more than 1 row for assignment to sobject single-record assignment from a query with several results add limit 1 or query into a list system.nullpointerexception: attempt to de-reference a null object calling a method or field on null — often a missing map key or unqueried parent ?. , ?? , containskey() , null checks sobject row was retrieved via soql without querying the requested field: account.industry reading a field you didn't select add it to the query system.listexception: list index out of bounds: 0 list[0] on an empty list check size first system.typeexception: invalid conversion from runtime type wrong cast (e.g. integer from a decimal aggregate) cast to the actual type; aggregates return decimal/integer depending on function maximum trigger depth exceeded recursion: a trigger keeps re-firing itself change detection and a processed-id set ( recursion control ) system.sobjectexception: field is not writeable setting a formula, roll-up, system or auto-number field don't set it; or set a different, editable field non-selective query against large object type trigger query on a big object without an indexed, selective filter filter on indexed fields; request a custom index ( selectivity )"},{"id":"dml-and-data-errors","h":"DML and data errors","t":"error cause fix field_custom_validation_exception a validation rule returned true the message after the code names the rule; fix the data or add a bypass for integrations required_field_missing a field required at field level isn't set set it; check the fields list in the error invalid_cross_reference_key an id points to a record that doesn't exist, is the wrong type, or you can't see check the id and its object; check sharing; don't use sandbox ids in production insufficient_access_or_readonly / insufficient_access_on_cross_reference_entity the user can't edit this record, or can't see a related record you're linking to check sharing on both records; run in system mode only if that's intended duplicate_value a unique or external id field already has this value upsert instead of insert; dedupe the input duplicates_detected a blocking duplicate rule matched merge, or use duplicateruleheader.allowsave where appropriate unable_to_lock_row another transaction holds a lock on the record or its parent (common with data skew and parallel loads) smaller batches, serial mode, sort child rows by parent, retry; fix skew mixed_dml_operation setup objects (user, group, permissionsetassignment) and normal objects in one transaction move one part to @future /queueable; in tests, wrap in system.runas entity_is_deleted updating a record that's in the recycle bin undelete first, or skip it string_too_long value exceeds field length truncate ( abbreviate ) or lengthen the field invalid_or_null_for_restricted_picklist picklist value not in a restricted list use the api value; add the value; check record type's allowed values cannot_insert_update_activate_entity a trigger or flow on the record threw an error read the rest of the message — it contains the real error and the trigger/flow name"},{"id":"callout-and-integration-errors","h":"Callout and integration errors","t":"error cause fix you have uncommitted work pending. please commit or rollback before calling out dml happened earlier in the transaction call out first, or move the callout to async callout from triggers are currently not supported synchronous callout in trigger context queueable / @future(callout=true) unauthorized endpoint, please check setup->security->remote site settings calling a url directly without a remote site setting use a named credential (no remote site needed) read timed out remote system too slow raise settimeout (max 120 s); go async; ask for a faster endpoint invalid_session_id (401) expired or revoked token re-authenticate / refresh the token request_limit_exceeded org's 24-hour api allowance used up find the chatty integration (setup → api usage); use bulk/composite; buy more api_disabled_for_org / api_currently_disabled edition without api, or the user lacks \"api enabled\" grant api enabled via permission set"},{"id":"deployment-errors","h":"Deployment errors","t":"error cause fix average test coverage across all apex classes and triggers is 72%, at least 75% test coverage is required not enough coverage org-wide add tests; check you're running the right test level test coverage of selected apex trigger is 0% a trigger in the deploy has no test add a test that performs dml on that object tests pass in sandbox, fail in production tests depend on org data ( seealldata ), hardcoded ids, or production-only validation rules/flows create all data in tests; use a test data factory no such column 'x__c' on entity / variable does not exist code references a field not in the deployment or target org include the field in the deploy, or deploy it first this component is in use by … deleting something still referenced remove references first, then use post-destructive changes cannot modify managed object trying to deploy changes to a managed package component exclude it (.forceignore); only subscriber-editable attributes can change this schedulable class has jobs pending or in progress deploying a class referenced by a scheduled job abort the job, deploy, reschedule — or enable deployments with pending jobs"},{"id":"flow-errors","h":"Flow errors","t":"error cause fix an unhandled fault has occurred in this flow an element failed with no fault path read the flow error email for the element and message; add a fault path too many soql queries: 101 (or dml) from a flow get/update elements inside a loop move dml/queries outside the loop; use collection updates the flow tried to update these records: null update records with an empty or null record variable decision: check the variable is not null before updating flow runs but nothing happens entry conditions not met; flow version inactive; wrong trigger type; running user lacks access debug the flow with the record; check active version and entry conditions"},{"id":"gotchas","h":"Gotchas","t":"\"it works for me\" you're a system administrator with view all data. test as the user (login as) and walk the security layers . 15- vs 18-character ids spreadsheets compare case-insensitively, so 15-char ids collide. use casesafeid(id) in a formula or the 18-char id from the api. hardcoded ids and urls differ between orgs. use developername lookups, custom metadata, named credentials, url.getorgdomainurl() . time zones datetimes are stored in utc. date values derived from datetime use the running user's zone; scheduled jobs use the org's default time zone. checkboxes are never null but booleans in apex can be. if (mybool) with null throws. formula fields aren't recalculated in apex memory after changing a field in apex, formula values on the in-memory record are stale until re-queried (or call formula.recalculateformulas(records) ). sandbox emails new sandboxes send \"system email only\". flows \"work\" but no email arrives until deliverability is changed. trigger order multiple triggers on one object run in no guaranteed order. keep one trigger per object. deleting a field deletes its data after 15 days in the recycle bin it's gone for good; export first. picklist api value vs label code and the api use api values; translations and renames change labels. compare against api values. release surprises read release notes and test release updates in a preview sandbox each release."}]},{"url":"events.html","title":"Platform Events & Change Data Capture","intro":"platform events & change data capture events decouple systems: a publisher says \"this happened\" without knowing who listens. platform events carry messages you define; change data capture streams record changes automatically.","sections":[{"id":"concepts","h":"Concepts","t":"flowchart lr subgraph publishers a1[apex / flow] a2[external system via api] end subgraph bus[\"salesforce event bus (retained ~72 hours)\"] pe[\"/event/order_shipped__e\"] cdc[\"/data/accountchangeevent\"] end subgraph subscribers s1[\"apex trigger\"] s2[\"platform event–triggered flow\"] s3[\"lwc (empapi)\"] s4[\"external service (pub/sub api)\"] end a1 --> pe a2 --> pe db[(record changes)] --> cdc pe --> s1 & s2 & s3 & s4 cdc --> s1 & s4 events are retained for 72 hours (high-volume events) and each has a replay id , so a subscriber that was offline can resume from where it left off. delivery is at least once and in order per channel — subscribers should be idempotent. events are not records: you can't query or update them, and they don't count toward storage."},{"id":"defining-a-platform-event","h":"Defining a platform event","t":"setup → platform events → new. name it like an object ( order_shipped__e ), add custom fields (text, number, checkbox, date/time, long text). choose the publish behaviour : publish behaviour delivered use when publish after commit only if the publishing transaction commits the event describes data that must exist (most cases) publish immediately right away, even if the transaction later rolls back logging and error reporting — e.g. log a failure that will cause a rollback"},{"id":"publishing","h":"Publishing","t":"list events = new list (); for (order__c o : shippedorders) { events.add(new order_shipped__e( order_number__c = o.name, carrier__c = o.carrier__c, shipped_at__c = datetime.now())); } list results = eventbus.publish(events); // one call for the list for (database.saveresult r : results) { if (!r.issuccess()) system.debug(logginglevel.error, r.geterrors()[0].getmessage()); } flow : a create records element on the platform event object publishes it. rest api : insert it like a record: curl -s -h \"$auth\" -h \"content-type: application/json\" -x post \\ \"$base/sobjects/order_shipped__e/\" -d '{\"order_number__c\":\"so-1001\",\"carrier__c\":\"ups\"}'"},{"id":"subscribing-in-salesforce","h":"Subscribing in Salesforce","t":"apex trigger trigger ordershippedtrigger on order_shipped__e (after insert) { // only after insert set numbers = new set (); for (order_shipped__e e : trigger.new) numbers.add(e.order_number__c); list orders = [select id, name from order__c where name in :numbers]; for (order__c o : orders) o.status__c = 'shipped'; update orders; // checkpoint: if a later failure retries, resume after the last processed event eventbus.triggercontext.currentcontext().setresumecheckpoint( trigger.new[trigger.new.size() - 1].replayid); } event triggers run in their own transaction as the automated process user (configurable with a platformeventsubscriberconfig to run as another user and set the batch size). batches can contain up to 2,000 events. throw eventbus.retryableexception to have the batch redelivered (up to 9 retries), e.g. when a lock or external system is temporarily unavailable. monitor subscribers in setup → platform events → the event → subscriptions (suspend, resume, see the last processed replay id). flow a platform event–triggered flow starts once per event message; $record is the event. good for simple reactions (create a task, update a record). lwc import { lightningelement } from 'lwc'; import { subscribe, unsubscribe, onerror } from 'lightning/empapi'; export default class shipmentticker extends lightningelement { subscription; async connectedcallback() { onerror(err => console.error('empapi error', json.stringify(err))); this.subscription = await subscribe('/event/order_shipped__e', -1, (msg) => { // -1 = new events only const p = msg.data.payload; this.latest = p.order_number__c + ' via ' + p.carrier__c; }); } disconnectedcallback() { unsubscribe(this.subscription); } }"},{"id":"subscribing-from-outside-pub-sub-api","h":"Subscribing from outside: Pub/Sub API","t":"the pub/sub api is a grpc api (http/2, avro-encoded payloads) for publishing and subscribing to platform events and cdc from external services. it replaces cometd/streaming api for new work. topics: /event/order_shipped__e , /data/accountchangeevent , /data/changeevents (all cdc-enabled objects), or a custom channel /data/my_channel__chn . subscribe is pull-based flow control: the client requests n events at a time. store the last processed replay id and pass it with replaypreset.custom when reconnecting. fetch the avro schema with getschema using the event's schema id. salesforce publishes sample clients in java, python, go and node.js."},{"id":"change-data-capture","h":"Change Data Capture","t":"setup → change data capture: select objects (standard and custom). salesforce then publishes a change event for every create, update, delete and undelete: accountchangeevent , invoice__changeevent . the change event header field meaning entityname object, e.g. account changetype create , update , delete , undelete , or gap_* / gap_overflow when details aren't available recordids records affected (several when changes are batched) changedfields fields changed in an update committimestamp , commitnumber , transactionkey , sequencenumber ordering and grouping within a transaction changeorigin , commituser which client/user made the change the event body contains only the fields that changed (plus the header) for updates; all non-null fields for creates. trigger accountchangetrigger on accountchangeevent (after insert) { set changedids = new set (); for (accountchangeevent e : trigger.new) { eventbus.changeeventheader h = e.changeeventheader; if (h.changetype != 'update') continue; for (string f : h.changedfields) { if (f.startswith('billing')) { changedids.addall(h.recordids); break; } } } if (!changedids.isempty()) { // e.g. re-geocode or notify an external system system.enqueuejob(new geocodejob(changedids)); } } cdc vs platform events vs triggers: cdc is automatic and ideal for keeping an external copy (data warehouse, search index) in sync. platform events are for business messages you design (\"order shipped\"). apex triggers on the object itself are synchronous and part of the saving transaction; cdc triggers run afterwards, separately, so they can't block or alter the save."},{"id":"testing","h":"Testing","t":"@istest static void shippedeventupdatesorder() { insert new order__c(name = 'so-1001', status__c = 'open'); test.starttest(); eventbus.publish(new order_shipped__e(order_number__c = 'so-1001')); test.stoptest(); // event delivered; trigger runs assert.areequal('shipped', [select status__c from order__c].status__c); } @istest static void cdctriggerruns() { test.enablechangedatacapture(); account a = new account(name = 'acme'); insert a; test.geteventbus().deliver(); // deliver the create change event a.billingstreet = '1 main st'; update a; test.geteventbus().deliver(); // deliver the update change event }"},{"id":"allocations-and-monitoring","h":"Allocations and monitoring","t":"publishing and delivery have hourly/daily allocations by edition (higher with add-on licences). check /limits ( hourlypublishedplatformevents , dailydeliveredplatformevents , cdc equivalents). delivery to apex triggers and flows doesn't count against the external delivery allocation. use event relays to forward events to amazon eventbridge without middleware. custom channels group events and add filters ( platformeventchannel , enriched fields for cdc)."}]},{"url":"flow.html","title":"Flow","intro":"flow flow is salesforce's declarative automation tool. it replaces workflow rules and process builder, and handles most automation that doesn't need code.","sections":[{"id":"flow-types","h":"Flow types","t":"type starts when runs as typical use record-triggered — fast field updates (before save) create/update, before the record is written system context set fields on the triggering record. no dml needed, very fast. record-triggered — actions and related records (after save) create/update/delete, after the record is written system context create/update other records, send email, call actions; scheduled and async paths. screen flow a user launches it: button, quick action, lightning page, utility bar, experience site user context (by default) wizards, guided data entry, multi-step forms. schedule-triggered a date/time, once or recurring; optionally for each record matching a filter system context nightly clean-up, reminders, batch updates. autolaunched (no trigger) called by apex, another flow, rest api, a button, or agentforce configurable reusable subflows and agent actions. platform event–triggered a platform event message is received automated process user react to integration events. orchestration record change or launch — multi-step, multi-user processes with stages and work items (e.g. onboarding approvals)."},{"id":"elements","h":"Elements","t":"element does notes get records queries records (soql) store first record or all records; choose fields. counts toward the 100-query limit. create / update / delete records dml pass a whole collection to do one dml for many records. counts toward the 150-dml limit. assignment sets variables; adds to collections operators: equals, add, subtract, add item, remove all… decision branches on conditions outcomes evaluated top-down; first match wins. has a default outcome. loop iterates a collection put assignments inside the loop; put dml after it. collection filter / collection sort filter or sort a collection in memory avoids extra queries. transform maps one collection/record shape to another replaces many loop + assignment patterns. subflow calls another flow pass inputs, receive outputs. build reusable pieces. action calls an action send email, post to chatter, submit for approval, invocable apex, external services, prompt templates, standard actions. screen shows a ui (screen flows only) standard components, reactive components, custom lwcs. wait / pause pauses until a time or event autolaunched/orchestration contexts. custom error shows an error and rolls back the save record-triggered flows — like a validation rule built in flow. roll back records undo pending changes in the transaction use in fault paths in screen flows."},{"id":"resources","h":"Resources","t":"variable text, number, currency, boolean, date, date/time, record (one sobject), apex-defined type; single or collection. tick available for input/output to pass values in and out. constant a fixed value. formula calculated on use, with the formula language: {!$record.amount} * 0.1 , today() + 7 . text template multi-line text with merge fields, plain or rich text — email bodies. choice / record choice set / collection choice set options for screen picklists and radio buttons. global variables $record , $record__prior , $user , $profile , $permission , $flow.currentdate , $flow.faultmessage , $label , $setup , $api.partner_server_url_xxx ."},{"id":"record-triggered-flows-in-detail","h":"Record-triggered flows in detail","t":"$record and $record__prior $record is the triggering record with its new values. in update-triggered flows, $record__prior holds the values before the change. compare them to act only on a real change: {!$record.stagename} = \"closed won\" && {!$record__prior.stagename} <> \"closed won\" parent fields are available without a get records: {!$record.account.owner.email} . entry conditions and \"when to run\" every time a record is updated and meets the condition requirements : runs on every save while the condition is true. only when a record is updated to meet the condition requirements : runs once when the record goes from not-matching to matching. usually what you want, and the only option that allows scheduled paths. use entry conditions instead of a first decision element: flows that don't match never start, which saves cpu. paths run immediately synchronous, inside the triggering transaction. run asynchronously after the transaction commits, in a separate transaction with its own limits. use for callouts and slow work. scheduled paths run at a time relative to a date field or the trigger time: \"3 days before contract end date\", \"1 hour after creation\". re-evaluated if the record changes. monitor in setup → time-based workflow / scheduled jobs. trigger order with several record-triggered flows on the same object and timing, set trigger order (1–2000) on each so they run in a known sequence. flows without one run after those with one, in an order you can't rely on. many teams keep one before-save and one after-save flow per object, with decisions inside, or use flow trigger explorer to see them all."},{"id":"fault-handling","h":"Fault handling","t":"any element that touches the database or calls an action can fail. without a fault path, the whole transaction rolls back, the user sees \"an unhandled fault has occurred\", and the admin (or the flow's \"last modified by\" user) gets an email. drag a fault connector from the element to a handler. log {!$flow.faultmessage} to a custom flow_error__c object or a platform event (platform events published \"immediately\" survive the rollback). in screen flows, show a friendly screen; in record-triggered flows, use custom error to show a clean message. set process automation settings → send process or flow error email to a monitored mailbox."},{"id":"calling-apex-from-flow","h":"Calling Apex from Flow","t":"when flow can't do something (complex parsing, a callout with logic, heavy calculations), expose apex as an invocable action . invocable methods are bulkified: flow passes a list of requests, one per flow interview in the batch. public with sharing class calculatetaxaction { public class request { @invocablevariable(label='amount' required=true) public decimal amount; @invocablevariable(label='country code' required=true) public string country; } public class result { @invocablevariable(label='tax') public decimal tax; @invocablevariable(label='rate') public decimal rate; } @invocablemethod(label='calculate tax' description='returns tax for an amount and country' category='finance') public static list calculate(list requests) { // one query for every interview in the batch map rates = new map (); for (tax_rate__mdt r : tax_rate__mdt.getall().values()) rates.put(r.country__c, r.rate__c); list results = new list (); for (request req : requests) { result res = new result(); res.rate = rates.containskey(req.country) ? rates.get(req.country) : 0; res.tax = (req.amount * res.rate).setscale(2); results.add(res); // results must be in the same order as requests } return results; } } launch a flow from apex: map inputs = new map { 'recordid' => acc.id, 'reason' => 'upgrade' }; flow.interview f = flow.interview.createinterview('account_upgrade', inputs); f.start(); string status = (string) f.getvariablevalue('resultstatus');"},{"id":"worked-examples","h":"Worked examples","t":"1. default a field before save goal: new opportunities get a close date 30 days out if blank, and a next step for the stage. record-triggered, object opportunity, a record is created , optimise for fast field updates . decision: {!$record.closedate} is null = true . assignment: {!$record.closedate} equals formula today() + 30 . no update element is needed: before-save flows write directly to $record . 2. create a follow-up task when a deal is won record-triggered, opportunity, created or updated , actions and related records. entry condition: stagename equals closed won ; run only when updated to meet the condition . create records: task with whatid = {!$record.id} , ownerid = {!$record.ownerid} , subject = \"kick-off call\" , activitydate = {!$flow.currentdate} + 2 (formula). fault path → create records on flow_error__c with {!$flow.faultmessage} . 3. update all contacts when an account's address changes record-triggered on account, updated, entry condition ischanged({!$record.billingstreet}) (use a formula entry condition). get records: contacts where accountid = {!$record.id} , all records. loop over contacts → assignment sets mailingstreet etc. on the loop variable → assignment add loop variable to contactstoupdate collection. after the loop: one update records with contactstoupdate . even simpler: an update records element can update \"records that meet conditions\" ( accountid = {!$record.id} ) and set fields directly, with no loop. 4. renewal reminder 60 days before contract end record-triggered on contract, created or updated, entry condition status = activated , run only when updated to meet the condition. scheduled path: 60 days before enddate . on that path: create a renewal opportunity and send an email alert to the account owner. 5. a screen flow to log a customer escalation screen flow with input variable recordid (text, available for input) — lightning pages pass the current record id automatically. get records: the case by recordid . screen: picklist for severity, long text for summary, a checkbox \"notify account owner\"; use conditional visibility to show a manager lookup only for severity = critical. update records: set escalated__c = true , priority from severity. decision on the checkbox → send email action. add the flow to the case page as a quick action (object manager → case → buttons, links and actions → new action → flow)."},{"id":"testing-and-debugging","h":"Testing and debugging","t":"debug button in flow builder: run with chosen inputs, as another user, with or without committing (rollback mode), and see every element's values. flow tests (record-triggered flows): define the triggering record and assertions, and run them from flow builder. apex tests that perform dml also execute your flows, so flow failures show up in apex test runs and deployments. debug logs record flow execution under the workflow category ( flow_element_begin , flow_element_error ). paused and failed flow interviews : setup → paused and failed flow interviews."},{"id":"versions-and-deployment","h":"Versions and deployment","t":"every save creates a new version ; only one version is active. running interviews (paused flows, scheduled paths) keep using the version they started on. deploy flows with change sets, devops center or the cli (metadata type flow ). in production, flows deploy as inactive unless \"deploy processes and flows as active\" is enabled in process automation settings — which then requires test coverage for record-triggered/autolaunched flows."},{"id":"best-practices","h":"Best practices","t":"practice why no get/create/update/delete inside a loop each iteration costs a query or dml; 101 records in a loop with a get records = limit exception. use before-save for same-record updates much faster; no extra dml; doesn't re-trigger automation. use entry conditions flows that don't match never start. fault paths on every dml/action clean errors instead of rollback emails. subflows for shared logic one place to change; smaller flows. don't hardcode ids use get records by developername, custom metadata, or custom labels. name elements clearly and fill in descriptions flows are read far more often than they're written. know when to switch to apex complex logic, heavy volume, or many interacting branches are easier to test and maintain in code. see which one do i pick?"},{"id":"migrating-workflow-rules-and-process-builder","h":"Migrating Workflow Rules and Process Builder","t":"setup → migrate to flow converts most workflow rules and processes into record-triggered flows. review each result: combine flows per object, convert same-record field updates to before-save, and test. deactivate the old automation once the new flow is live."},{"id":"approvals","h":"Approvals","t":"approval processes (setup → approval processes) define entry criteria, approvers (manager, a user, a queue, a related user field), steps, record locking, and actions on submit, approve, reject and recall. submit from a button, flow (submit for approval action) or apex ( approval.process(new approval.processsubmitrequest()) ). for multi-stage approvals with parallel approvers and richer ui, use flow approval processes built on orchestration."}]},{"url":"formulas.html","title":"Formulas & validation rules","intro":"formulas & validation rules the same excel-like formula language powers formula fields, validation rules, default values, flow formulas, report formulas and approval criteria. learn it once, use it everywhere.","sections":[{"id":"syntax-basics","h":"Syntax basics","t":"fields by api name: amount , discount__c . parent fields with dots: account.industry , invoice__r.status__c . operators: + - * / ^ , & (concatenate text), = == != <> < > <= >= , && and || (or the functions and() , or() ). text in double quotes: \"closed won\" . comments: /* like this */ . picklists can't be compared directly: use ispickval(stagename, \"closed won\") or text(stagename) = \"closed won\" . global variables: $user.id , $user.department , $profile.name , $userrole.name , $permission.my_custom_permission , $organization.name , $setup.my_setting__c.field__c , $custommetadata.type__mdt.record.field__c , $label.my_label . blank handling: number formulas can treat blank fields as zero or as blank (an option on the formula field). blankvalue(field, default) makes it explicit. limits: 3,900 characters of formula text and 5,000 bytes compiled size (formulas that reference other formulas count their compiled size too)."},{"id":"function-reference","h":"Function reference","t":"category functions logic if(test, then, else) , case(expr, v1, r1, v2, r2, …, else) , and , or , not , isblank , blankvalue , nullvalue , isnumber picklists ispickval(field, \"value\") , text(picklist) , includes(multiselect, \"value\") , priorvalue , ischanged change detection (validation rules, flows, defaults) isnew() , ischanged(field) , priorvalue(field) , isclone() text len , left , right , mid , find , contains , begins , substitute , trim , upper , lower , lpad , rpad , regex , text , value , br() , hyperlink(url, label) , image(url, alt) , casesafeid math round , ceiling , floor , mod , abs , max , min , sqrt , exp , ln , log , distance , geolocation date & time today() , now() , date(y,m,d) , datevalue(datetime) , datetimevalue , year , month , day , weekday (1 = sunday), addmonths , isoweek , isoyear , timevalue , hour , minute , unixtimestamp lookups (validation rules only) vlookup(field_to_return, field_on_lookup_object, lookup_value) , regex"},{"id":"formula-field-examples","h":"Formula field examples","t":"if(isclosed, datevalue(closeddate) - datevalue(createddate), today() - datevalue(createddate)) if(amount >= 1000000, \"enterprise\", if(amount >= 100000, \"large\", if(amount >= 10000, \"medium\", \"small\"))) case(text(priority), \"critical\", 100, \"high\", 70, \"medium\", 40, 10) + if(account.type = \"strategic\", 25, 0) image( case(text(health__c), \"green\", \"/img/samples/light_green.gif\", \"yellow\", \"/img/samples/light_yellow.gif\", \"/img/samples/light_red.gif\"), text(health__c), 16, 16) hyperlink(\"https://erp.example.com/customers/\" & account.erp_id__c, \"open in erp\", \"_blank\") /* weekdays between start__c and end__c. 1900-01-08 is a monday, used as an anchor. */ (5 * floor((end__c - date(1900, 1, 8)) / 7) + min(5, mod(end__c - date(1900, 1, 8), 7))) - (5 * floor((start__c - date(1900, 1, 8)) / 7) + min(5, mod(start__c - date(1900, 1, 8), 7))) trim(blankvalue(salutation & \" \", \"\") & firstname & \" \" & lastname) addmonths(closedate, 12) round((now() - createddate) * 24, 1) formula fields are calculated when read. they don't fire triggers or flows when their value \"changes\", they can't be indexed (unless deterministic and approved by salesforce support), and cross-object formulas add query cost. if you need to trigger automation or filter big tables on a value, store it in a real field with flow or apex."},{"id":"validation-rules","h":"Validation rules","t":"a validation rule is a formula that returns true when the data is invalid . when it's true, the save fails with your error message, shown at the top of the page or next to a field. when they run on every save — ui, api, apex, flow, data loader — after before-triggers (see order of execution ). what they can use the record's fields, parent fields, isnew() , ischanged() , priorvalue() , $user , $permission , vlookup , regex . what they can't do look at child records, query, or check other records being saved in the same batch. use apex for that. examples and( isblank(amount), or(ispickval(stagename, \"proposal/price quote\"), ispickval(stagename, \"negotiation/review\"), ispickval(stagename, \"closed won\")) ) and(ispickval(stagename, \"closed lost\"), isblank(text(loss_reason__c))) and(not(isclosed), closedate /* compare positions in an ordered list of stages */ and( ischanged(stagename), case(text(stagename), \"prospecting\",1, \"qualification\",2, \"proposal/price quote\",3, \"negotiation/review\",4, \"closed won\",5, 0) and( not(isblank(phone)), billingcountry = \"us\", not(regex(phone, \"\\\\d*?(\\\\d\\\\d*?){10}\")) ) and( not(isblank(email)), not(isblank(account.website)), not(contains(lower(account.website), lower(mid(email, find(\"@\", email) + 1, 255)))) ) and( ischanged(discount__c), ispickval(approval_status__c, \"approved\"), not($permission.edit_approved_discounts) ) and( not(isnew()), ispickval(priorvalue(status), \"closed\"), not(ischanged(status)), /* still allow reopening */ not($permission.edit_closed_cases) ) and( billingcountry = \"us\", /* zip_code__c records: name = the 5-digit code, city__c = the city */ vlookup($objecttype.zip_code__c.fields.city__c, $objecttype.zip_code__c.fields.name, left(billingpostalcode, 5)) <> billingcity ) and(not(isblank(end_date__c)), end_date__c validation rule best practices write the error for the user : say what to do, not what's wrong. \"enter a loss reason before closing the opportunity as lost.\" put the error on the field the user needs to fix when there is one. add a bypass : not($permission.bypass_validation) with a custom permission assigned to integration and data-migration users via a permission set. much cleaner than checking profile names. mind data loads : activating a new rule doesn't check existing records, but the next edit of any old record will fail if it's invalid. test with apex : validation rules fire in tests, so test data must satisfy them. keep a test data factory up to date."},{"id":"default-values-and-other-formula-uses","h":"Default values and other formula uses","t":"where example field default value today() + 30 on a due date field; $user.region__c on a region field flow formula resource {!$record.amount} * 0.1 (flow uses {!...} merge syntax) report row-level formula if(amount > 100000, \"big\", \"normal\") report summary formula won:sum / amount:sum (win rate by group) approval process entry criteria discount__c > 0.2 button/link url /lightning/o/case/new?defaultfieldvalues=accountid={!account.id}"}]},{"url":"fundamentals.html","title":"Platform fundamentals","intro":"platform fundamentals the handful of ideas that explain most of salesforce's behaviour, including the parts that surprise developers coming from ordinary web stacks.","sections":[{"id":"multi-tenancy","h":"Multi-tenancy","t":"salesforce runs thousands of customers on shared infrastructure. each customer's slice is an org (organisation): its own data, configuration, users and code, isolated logically rather than physically. orgs live on instances (server pods with names like na224 or eu46 ) in salesforce's data centres or on hyperforce, its public-cloud infrastructure. because tenants share cpu, memory and database capacity, the platform enforces governor limits : hard caps per transaction (100 soql queries, 150 dml statements, 10 seconds of cpu…) and per org per day (api calls, async jobs, emails). limits are the single biggest difference between salesforce development and general web development. see governor limits . consequence: code that works for one record can fail for 200. everything on the platform, including flow, must be written for batches of records."},{"id":"everything-is-metadata","h":"Everything is metadata","t":"an object, a field, a page layout, a flow, an apex class, a permission set and a report are all metadata : xml descriptions the platform reads at runtime. creating a custom field in setup doesn't run a database migration you control; it writes metadata, and the platform adapts. metadata can be retrieved to files, stored in git and deployed to another org — that's how changes move from sandbox to production ( devops ). data (records) and metadata (configuration) move separately. deploying a picklist doesn't move the records that use it. some settings are data-like but deployable: custom metadata types ( __mdt ) let you ship configuration rows with your code. amount__c amount currency 16 2 false true that file is exactly what setup creates when you add a currency field called amount. clicks and code are two editors for the same metadata."},{"id":"orgs-editions-and-licences","h":"Orgs, editions and licences","t":"edition who uses it notes developer edition learning, prototyping, isv development free, permanent, small data and user limits. not connected to any company. starter / pro suite small businesses bundled, simplified editions with limited customisation. professional small to mid-size limited api and automation unless add-ons are purchased. enterprise most companies full api, apex, sandboxes, profiles and permission sets. the usual baseline. unlimited large companies enterprise plus higher limits, more sandboxes, premier support. separately from edition, each user has a user licence (salesforce, salesforce platform, salesforce integration, customer community, partner community…) that sets which objects and features they can ever use, and optional permission set licences for add-on features. a platform licence user, for example, cannot access opportunities or cases regardless of permissions."},{"id":"environments","h":"Environments","t":"environment contains refresh interval use for production real users and data — running the business. you can't write apex directly in production; you deploy it. developer sandbox metadata only (small storage) 1 day individual development developer pro sandbox metadata only (larger storage) 1 day development with more test data, integration testing partial copy sandbox metadata + a sample of data (via a sandbox template) 5 days qa, testing with realistic data full sandbox metadata + all data 29 days uat, performance testing, staging scratch org built from your source code and a definition file disposable (1–30 days) source-driven development, ci, package development sandboxes are copies of production made at refresh time; refreshing wipes the sandbox . users are copied too, with .sandboxname appended to usernames and email addresses scrambled with .invalid so you don't email real customers. log in to sandboxes at test.salesforce.com or via the my domain url. post-refresh tasks are common: reset integration endpoints, fix user emails, seed test data. automate them with an apex class implementing sandboxpostcopy , which runs automatically after a sandbox is created or refreshed. global class sandboxsetup implements sandboxpostcopy { global void runapexclass(sandboxcontext context) { // context.sandboxname() tells you which sandbox this is, e.g. 'uat' // org_switch__c is a hierarchy custom setting; turn outbound integrations off // so a sandbox never calls production systems. insert new org_switch__c(setupownerid = userinfo.getorganizationid(), disable_callouts__c = true); // point integration users at a safe mailbox list integrationusers = [select id, email from user where profile.name = 'integration user']; for (user u : integrationusers) u.email = 'sandbox-alerts@example.com'; update integrationusers; } }"},{"id":"my-domain-and-urls","h":"My Domain and URLs","t":"every org has a my domain , e.g. acme.my.salesforce.com . sandboxes look like acme--uat.sandbox.my.salesforce.com . you'll see these url shapes: url what it is https://acme.my.salesforce.com login and api base url https://acme.lightning.force.com/lightning/r/account/001.../view a record page in lightning experience https://acme.lightning.force.com/lightning/o/account/list an object's list view https://acme.lightning.force.com/lightning/setup/setuponehome/home setup home https://acme.my.salesforce.com/services/data/v64.0/ rest api root (version varies) https://acme.my.site.com experience cloud sites"},{"id":"record-ids","h":"Record IDs","t":"every record has an id : 15 characters case-sensitive (shown in urls and older reports) or 18 characters case-insensitive (returned by apis and apex). the first three characters are the key prefix and identify the object. prefix object prefix object 001 account 005 user 003 contact 00g group / queue 006 opportunity 00e profile 00q lead 0ps permission set 500 case 00t / 00u task / event a00 – a0z … custom objects (assigned per org) 01p apex class id recid = '001000000000001aaa'; schema.sobjecttype t = recid.getsobjecttype(); // account string objname = t.getdescribe().getname(); // 'account' boolean isaccount = recid.getsobjecttype() == account.sobjecttype; string prefix = account.sobjecttype.getdescribe().getkeyprefix(); // '001' string id18 = string.valueof(recid); // apex always uses 18-char ids // in formulas: casesafeid(id) converts to 18 characters never hardcode ids. record ids differ between sandbox and production (except for records copied in a full/partial refresh, which keep the production id at refresh time). look records up by developername , an external id, or a custom metadata record."},{"id":"releases-and-api-versions","h":"Releases and API versions","t":"salesforce ships three major releases a year — spring (feb), summer (jun) and winter (oct, named for the following year). sandboxes on preview instances get each release about a month early. each release adds an api version (winter '26 was 65.0; each release adds 1.0). every apex class, trigger, lwc and flow stores the api version it was saved with. behaviour is versioned: old code keeps old behaviour until you bump its version. api clients choose a version in the url: /services/data/v64.0/ . release updates (setup → release updates) are behaviour changes with an enforcement date. test and enable them in a sandbox before they're forced on. features are retired on published schedules. workflow rules and process builder reached end of support on 31 december 2025; existing ones still run but get no fixes."},{"id":"lightning-experience-vs-classic","h":"Lightning Experience vs Classic","t":"lightning experience is the current ui: component-based pages built in lightning app builder, lwc, dynamic forms. salesforce classic is the pre-2016 ui; it still exists and some old orgs and visualforce pages depend on it, but new features are lightning-only. when documentation shows two sets of instructions, use the lightning ones."},{"id":"navigating-setup","h":"Navigating Setup","t":"gear icon (top right) → setup . the quick find box at the top of the left sidebar is the fastest way anywhere. the places you'll visit most: quick find term what's there object manager (top tab, not quick find.) every object's fields, relationships, layouts, record types, validation rules, triggers, buttons. users create, freeze, deactivate users; reset passwords; see a user's permission summary. permission sets / permission set groups / profiles access control. sharing settings org-wide defaults and sharing rules. flows all flows, versions, and the flow builder. apex classes / apex triggers / apex jobs code, and the status of async jobs. debug logs trace flags for capturing execution logs. deployment status every deployment, its test results and errors. company information org id, edition, licences, storage. system overview usage against key limits (api calls, storage, code). view setup audit trail who changed what in setup in the last 180 days."},{"id":"storage","h":"Storage","t":"orgs have two storage pools. data storage counts records (most records count as 2 kb regardless of size). file storage counts files, attachments and documents. check usage in setup → storage usage. running out blocks inserts, so archive old records and keep large binaries out of salesforce when you can."}]},{"url":"glossary.html","title":"Glossary","intro":"glossary platform vocabulary in one place. type to filter. a agentforce salesforce's platform for building ai agents that take actions. anonymous apex apex run once without saving a class (developer console or sf apex run). apex salesforce's server-side, java-like programming language. apex managed sharing sharing records programmatically by inserting rows into __share objects. apex rest custom rest endpoints written in apex with @restresource. api name the name used in code and the api (invoice__c), as opposed to the label shown to users. api version versioned platform behaviour; each release adds one (e.g., 64.0). set per class/component. appexchange salesforce's app marketplace. aura the older lightning component framework. auraenabled annotation exposing an apex method to lightning components. b batch apex async apex that processes large record sets in chunks, each with fresh limits. before-save flow a record-triggered flow that updates the triggering record before it's saved, without dml. bulk api 2.0 an asynchronous rest api for loading and extracting large volumes of records. bulkification writing code that handles many records at once without per-record queries or dml. business hours working-hour calendars used by escalation rules, milestones and businesshours in apex. c cdc change data capture: an event stream of record changes. change data capture streaming record changes as events for external systems to consume. change set a ui-based bundle of metadata deployed between connected orgs. code coverage percentage of apex lines executed by tests; 75% required for production deploys. composite api a rest resource that runs several requests in one call, optionally all-or-none. connected app / external client app an oauth client definition for external systems calling salesforce. custom metadata type deployable configuration records (__mdt). custom permission a named flag granted through permission sets and checked in formulas, flow, apex and lwc. custom setting","sections":[]},{"url":"index.html","title":"Salesforce Field Guide","intro":"salesforce field guide a reference for the salesforce platform, written for admins and developers. each chapter is its own page with worked examples you can paste into an org. use the sidebar, the chapter cards below, or press / to search every page. practise as you read. a free developer edition org is a full salesforce org that never expires. every example in this guide works in one.","sections":[{"id":"foundations","h":"Foundations","t":"platform fundamentals multi-tenancy, metadata, orgs and editions, sandboxes, record ids, releases, api versions, where things live in setup. data model standard objects, every field type, lookup vs master-detail, junction objects, record types, layouts, schema naming. formulas & validation rules formula syntax, the functions you'll use daily, 30+ worked formula and validation rule examples. security & sharing profiles, permission sets, fls, org-wide defaults, role hierarchy, sharing rules, apex managed sharing, debugging access."},{"id":"administration","h":"Administration","t":"admin essentials users and licences, lightning apps and pages, data import tools, duplicates, queues, email, audit and debug logs. reports & dashboards report formats, report types, filters, buckets, summary formulas, joined reports, dashboards and subscriptions. sales & service processes lead conversion, opportunities, products and price books, forecasting; cases, routing, omni-channel, entitlements, knowledge."},{"id":"automation","h":"Automation","t":"flow flow types, every element, $record and $record__prior, scheduled and async paths, fault handling, five worked flows. order of execution what runs, in what order, when a record is saved — and how to use it to explain surprising behaviour."},{"id":"development","h":"Development","t":"apex language types, collections, classes, interfaces, exceptions, dml, json, dates, strings, user mode. triggers context variables, the handler pattern, recursion control, bypass switches, six real trigger examples. asynchronous apex queueable, future, batch, schedulable, finalizers, chaining, monitoring jobs. testing apex test data factories, assert, runas, mocking callouts and dependencies, testing async and exceptions. soql & sosl filters, relationships, aggregates, date literals, polymorphism, locking, dynamic soql, search. governor limits & performance every per-transaction limit, bulkification patterns, selective queries, large data volumes, data skew. lightning web components templates, lifecycle, reactivity, events, lightning data service, apex, navigation, modals, message service, jest."},{"id":"integration-and-delivery","h":"Integration & delivery","t":"apis & callouts rest, composite, bulk api 2.0, apex rest services, oauth flows, named credentials, callouts with retry. platform events & cdc defining, publishing and subscribing to events; change data capture; the pub/sub api; replay and retries. devops & the sf cli project files, sandboxes and scratch orgs, deployments, destructive changes, packages, a ci pipeline."},{"id":"products-and-reference","h":"Products & reference","t":"product catalog clouds, platform features and add-ons: what each is and when it's used. agentforce & data 360 agents, topics and actions, prompt templates, the trust layer; data streams, unified profiles and segments. which one do i pick? decision tables: flow vs apex, lookup vs master-detail, config storage, async types, data tools, integrations. errors & gotchas the error messages you'll see most, what they mean, and how to fix them. glossary platform vocabulary from account to wire adapter. docs & certifications official documentation, trailhead, certification paths and a suggested reading order for this guide."},{"id":"how-salesforce-fits-together","h":"How Salesforce fits together","t":"salesforce is a hosted database with an application server, a security model, an automation engine and a ui framework layered on top. all of it is configured through metadata , which is why \"clicks\" (setup screens) and \"code\" (apex, lwc) can build the same things. flowchart tb ui[\"user interface lightning pages · lwc · mobile · experience sites\"] auto[\"business logic flow · validation rules · apex triggers & classes\"] sec[\"security profiles · permission sets · sharing\"] data[(\"data standard & custom objects\")] api[\"apis rest · bulk · events · pub/sub\"] ui --> sec api --> sec sec --> auto auto --> data every request, whether from a person clicking save or an integration calling the api, goes through the same security checks and the same automation before it reaches the database. that shared path is why a trigger written for the ui also runs for a data load of 50,000 records, and why governor limits matter so much."}]},{"url":"integration.html","title":"APIs & callouts","intro":"apis & callouts inbound, other systems call salesforce's apis. outbound, salesforce calls other systems (callouts) or publishes events they subscribe to ( platform events & cdc ).","sections":[{"id":"choosing-a-pattern","h":"Choosing a pattern","t":"need pattern tools another app reads/writes a few records in real time request–reply, inbound rest api, composite api, graphql api load or extract thousands to millions of records batch data sync bulk api 2.0, data loader, etl tools custom endpoint with business logic request–reply, inbound apex rest ( @restresource ) salesforce needs data from another system right now request–reply, outbound apex callouts, external services (from flow) tell other systems something happened fire-and-forget / pub-sub platform events, change data capture, pub/sub api show external data without copying it data virtualisation salesforce connect (external objects) many systems, many apis, governance middleware mulesoft or another integration platform"},{"id":"authentication-for-inbound-calls","h":"Authentication for inbound calls","t":"external systems authenticate with oauth 2.0 against an app definition in salesforce: an external client app (the current model) or a connected app (older, still widespread). the app holds the consumer key/secret, allowed flows, scopes, and policies (which users, ip relaxation, token lifetime). flow use when client credentials server-to-server; the app runs as a designated \"run as\" integration user. simplest for back-end jobs. jwt bearer server-to-server with a certificate; no secret sent. common in ci ( sf org login jwt ). web server (authorization code + pkce) an app where a real user logs in and consents. refresh token keep access without re-login after an authorization-code flow. username-password legacy; avoid (blocked by default in new orgs). curl -s https://acme.my.salesforce.com/services/oauth2/token \\ -d grant_type=client_credentials \\ -d client_id=\"$sf_client_id\" \\ -d client_secret=\"$sf_client_secret\" # → {\"access_token\":\"00d...!aq...\",\"instance_url\":\"https://acme.my.salesforce.com\",\"token_type\":\"bearer\",...} # one-time: create a certificate, upload server.crt to the app, pre-authorise the user's profile/permission set openssl req -x509 -newkey rsa:2048 -nodes -keyout server.key -out server.crt -days 730 -subj \"/cn=ci\" sf org login jwt --client-id \"$sf_client_id\" --jwt-key-file server.key \\ --username ci-user@acme.com --instance-url https://login.salesforce.com --alias prod integration users: give each integration its own user on the free salesforce integration licence (api-only), with a permission set granting only what it needs. you can then see, limit and revoke each integration separately."},{"id":"rest-api","h":"REST API","t":"base url: https://<my-domain>.my.salesforce.com/services/data/vxx.x/ . send authorization: bearer <token> . json in and out. base=https://acme.my.salesforce.com/services/data/v64.0 auth=\"authorization: bearer $token\" # query (url-encode the soql); follow nextrecordsurl for more than 2,000 rows curl -s -h \"$auth\" \"$base/query?q=select+id,name+from+account+where+industry='energy'+limit+10\" # get one record (optionally choose fields) curl -s -h \"$auth\" \"$base/sobjects/account/001xxxxxxxxxxxx?fields=name,industry\" # create → 201 {\"id\":\"001...\",\"success\":true,\"errors\":[]} curl -s -h \"$auth\" -h \"content-type: application/json\" -x post \"$base/sobjects/account/\" \\ -d '{\"name\":\"acme\",\"industry\":\"energy\"}' # update → 204 no content curl -s -h \"$auth\" -h \"content-type: application/json\" -x patch \"$base/sobjects/account/001xxxxxxxxxxxx\" \\ -d '{\"rating\":\"hot\"}' # upsert by external id → 201 created or 200/204 updated curl -s -h \"$auth\" -h \"content-type: application/json\" -x patch \"$base/sobjects/account/erp_id__c/erp-1001\" \\ -d '{\"name\":\"acme ltd\"}' # delete → 204 curl -s -h \"$auth\" -x delete \"$base/sobjects/account/001xxxxxxxxxxxx\" # metadata about an object (fields, picklists, relationships) curl -s -h \"$auth\" \"$base/sobjects/account/describe\" # org limits, including remaining daily api calls curl -s -h \"$auth\" \"$base/limits\" composite and sobject collections save round trips and api calls by batching: { \"allornone\": true, \"compositerequest\": [ { \"method\": \"post\", \"url\": \"/services/data/v64.0/sobjects/account\", \"referenceid\": \"newacct\", \"body\": { \"name\": \"acme\" } }, { \"method\": \"post\", \"url\": \"/services/data/v64.0/sobjects/contact\", \"referenceid\": \"newcontact\", \"body\": { \"lastname\": \"lovelace\", \"accountid\": \"@{newacct.id}\" } }, { \"method\": \"get\", \"referenceid\": \"check\", \"url\": \"/services/data/v64.0/sobjects/account/@{newacct.id}?fields=name\" } ] } { \"allornone\": false, \"records\": [ { \"attributes\": { \"type\": \"account\" }, \"name\": \"north\" }, { \"attributes\": { \"type\": \"account\" }, \"name\": \"south\" }, { \"attributes\": { \"type\": \"contact\" }, \"lastname\": \"turing\" } ] } also available: patch /composite/sobjects (update), patch /composite/sobjects/account/erp_id__c (upsert many by external id), delete /composite/sobjects?ids=… , /composite/tree (parent with nested children), and /composite/graph (several independent all-or-none graphs). error responses [ { \"message\": \"required fields are missing: [lastname]\", \"errorcode\": \"required_field_missing\", \"fields\": [\"lastname\"] } ] http 400 for bad input, 401 for expired tokens (re-authenticate), 403 for permissions or api disabled, 404 for missing records, 503/ request_limit_exceeded when the org is out of api calls."},{"id":"bulk-api-2-0","h":"Bulk API 2.0","t":"for large volumes: upload csv, salesforce processes it asynchronously in parallel batches, you download success and failure files. # 1. create the job job=$(curl -s -h \"$auth\" -h \"content-type: application/json\" -x post \"$base/jobs/ingest\" \\ -d '{\"object\":\"account\",\"operation\":\"upsert\",\"externalidfieldname\":\"erp_id__c\",\"contenttype\":\"csv\",\"lineending\":\"lf\"}' \\ | jq -r .id) # 2. upload the data (up to 150 mb per job after base64 encoding) curl -s -h \"$auth\" -h \"content-type: text/csv\" -x put \"$base/jobs/ingest/$job/batches\" --data-binary @accounts.csv # 3. tell salesforce the upload is complete curl -s -h \"$auth\" -h \"content-type: application/json\" -x patch \"$base/jobs/ingest/$job\" -d '{\"state\":\"uploadcomplete\"}' # 4. poll until state is jobcomplete (or failed) curl -s -h \"$auth\" \"$base/jobs/ingest/$job\" | jq '{state, numberrecordsprocessed, numberrecordsfailed}' # 5. results curl -s -h \"$auth\" \"$base/jobs/ingest/$job/successfulresults/\" > ok.csv curl -s -h \"$auth\" \"$base/jobs/ingest/$job/failedresults/\" > failed.csv # includes sf__error column qj=$(curl -s -h \"$auth\" -h \"content-type: application/json\" -x post \"$base/jobs/query\" \\ -d '{\"operation\":\"query\",\"query\":\"select id, name, industry from account\"}' | jq -r .id) # poll $base/jobs/query/$qj until jobcomplete, then page through results: curl -s -h \"$auth\" \"$base/jobs/query/$qj/results?maxrecords=50000\" -d headers.txt > part1.csv # repeat with &locator= until the locator is \"null\" the cli wraps this: sf data upsert bulk --sobject account --file accounts.csv --external-id erp_id__c --wait 10 and sf data export bulk --query \"…\" --output-file out.csv --wait 10 ."},{"id":"apex-rest-services","h":"Apex REST services","t":"expose your own endpoint at /services/apexrest/… when the standard api isn't the right shape. @restresource(urlmapping='/orders/*') global with sharing class orderapi { global class orderrequest { public string accounterpid; public list lines; } global class linerequest { public string sku; public integer qty; } @httpget global static void getorder() { restrequest req = restcontext.request; restresponse res = restcontext.response; string ordernumber = req.requesturi.substringafterlast('/'); list found = [select id, name, status__c, total__c from order__c where name = :ordernumber with user_mode limit 1]; res.addheader('content-type', 'application/json'); if (found.isempty()) { res.statuscode = 404; res.responsebody = blob.valueof('{\"error\":\"order not found\"}'); return; } res.responsebody = blob.valueof(json.serialize(found[0])); } @httppost global static void createorder() { restresponse res = restcontext.response; res.addheader('content-type', 'application/json'); try { orderrequest body = (orderrequest) json.deserialize( restcontext.request.requestbody.tostring(), orderrequest.class); order__c o = new order__c(account__r = new account(erp_id__c = body.accounterpid)); insert as user o; res.statuscode = 201; res.responsebody = blob.valueof(json.serialize(new map { 'id' => o.id })); } catch (jsonexception e) { res.statuscode = 400; res.responsebody = blob.valueof('{\"error\":\"invalid json\"}'); } catch (dmlexception e) { res.statuscode = 422; res.responsebody = blob.valueof(json.serialize(new map { 'error' => e.getdmlmessage(0) })); } } } curl -s -h \"$auth\" https://acme.my.salesforce.com/services/apexrest/orders/so-1001 curl -s -h \"$auth\" -h \"content-type: application/json\" -x post https://acme.my.salesforce.com/services/apexrest/orders \\ -d '{\"accounterpid\":\"erp-1001\",\"lines\":[{\"sku\":\"w-1\",\"qty\":2}]}' test it by setting restcontext.request and restcontext.response in a test method, then calling the static method."},{"id":"outbound-callouts","h":"Outbound callouts","t":"named and external credentials external credential : the authentication protocol (oauth 2.0 client credentials, jwt, custom headers / api key, aws signature v4) and its principals (the actual secrets, per-user or shared). named credential : the endpoint url plus a reference to the external credential. code calls callout:name/path . grant users (including the integration or automated process user) access to the external credential principal via a permission set . no secrets or urls in code, nothing to change in code between sandbox and production, and no remote site settings needed. public with sharing class billingclient { public class billingexception extends exception {} public static list > invoicesfor(string customerid) { httprequest req = new httprequest(); req.setendpoint('callout:billing_api/v1/invoices?customer=' + encodingutil.urlencode(customerid, 'utf-8')); req.setmethod('get'); req.setheader('accept', 'application/json'); req.settimeout(20000); // ms; max 120,000 per callout httpresponse res; try { res = new http().send(req); } catch (calloutexception e) { // timeout, dns, tls problems throw new billingexception('billing api unreachable: ' + e.getmessage(), e); } integer code = res.getstatuscode(); if (code == 200) { map body = (map ) json.deserializeuntyped(res.getbody()); list > out = new list >(); for (object o : (list ) body.get('invoices')) out.add((map ) o); return out; } if (code == 404) return new list >(); throw new billingexception('billing api returned ' + code + ': ' + res.getbody().abbreviate(500)); } } callout rules no callouts after uncommitted dml in the same transaction ( you have uncommitted work pending ). call out first, or move the callout to a queueable / @future(callout=true) / async flow path. no synchronous callouts from triggers. enqueue async work from the trigger instead. up to 100 callouts per transaction and 120 s total; responses up to 6 mb sync / 12 mb async (heap). continuations let lwc/visualforce controllers make long-running callouts without holding a server thread. from flow, use http callout actions or external services (register an openapi spec; operations become flow actions). retries public class retryingsyncjob implements queueable, database.allowscallouts { private final id recordid; private final integer attempt; public retryingsyncjob(id recordid, integer attempt) { this.recordid = recordid; this.attempt = attempt; } public void execute(queueablecontext ctx) { httpresponse res = erpclient.push(recordid); integer code = res.getstatuscode(); if (code == 429 || code >= 500) { if (attempt"},{"id":"other-integration-tools","h":"Other integration tools","t":"graphql api post /services/data/vxx.x/graphql — fetch exactly the fields and related records a client needs in one query. also available to lwc as a wire adapter. soap api older, wsdl-based (enterprise and partner wsdls). still used by some middleware and data loader. metadata & tooling apis deploy and inspect metadata; used by the cli and ides. outbound messages legacy soap notifications from workflow rules. prefer platform events or flows with http callouts. salesforce connect external objects backed by odata 2.0/4.0, cross-org, or custom apex adapters. queried live; count against callout limits. heroku / aws / mulesoft connectors common for heavy processing outside salesforce."}]},{"url":"limits.html","title":"Governor limits & performance","intro":"governor limits & performance every transaction gets a fixed budget. exceed any of it and salesforce throws system.limitexception , which can't be caught, and rolls back the entire transaction.","sections":[{"id":"per-transaction-apex-limits","h":"Per-transaction Apex limits","t":"limit synchronous asynchronous what usually breaks it soql queries 100 200 queries inside loops; many flows/triggers on one object rows returned by soql 50,000 50,000 unfiltered queries; aggregates over big tables sosl queries 20 20 search inside loops dml statements 150 150 insert/update inside loops rows processed by dml 10,000 10,000 mass updates — use batch apex cpu time 10,000 ms 60,000 ms nested loops, string building, heavy automation stacks heap size 6 mb 12 mb large query results, big json, file bodies in memory callouts 100 100 one callout per record total callout timeout 120 s 120 s slow apis @future calls 50 0 in batch and future a future call per record queueable jobs enqueued 50 1 enqueuing per record messaging.sendemail calls 10 10 one send per record — build a list instead published platform events (immediate) 150 eventbus.publish calls 150 publishing per record trigger/flow recursion depth 16 16 triggers updating records that fire themselves max transaction execution time 10 minutes 10 minutes long-running callouts and queries flows count against the same limits: each get records is a soql query, each create/update/delete is a dml statement. managed packages have their own separate soql/dml/cpu counters per namespace (cpu and heap are shared)."},{"id":"bulkification","h":"Bulkification","t":"a trigger, flow or invocable action receives up to 200 records at once. code must use a constant number of queries and dml statements regardless of how many records arrive. pattern: collect → query once → map → loop → dml once for (contact c : trigger.new) { account a = [select id, tier__c from account where id = :c.accountid]; // query per record c.tier__c = a.tier__c; } set accountids = new set (); for (contact c : trigger.new) accountids.add(c.accountid); map accounts = new map ( [select id, tier__c from account where id in :accountids]); for (contact c : trigger.new) { account a = accounts.get(c.accountid); if (a != null) c.tier__c = a.tier__c; } for (opportunity o : wonopps) { insert new task(whatid = o.id, subject = 'kick-off'); // 150 won deals = limit } list tasks = new list (); for (opportunity o : wonopps) tasks.add(new task(whatid = o.id, subject = 'kick-off')); insert tasks; pattern: avoid nested loops with maps for (opportunity o : opps) { for (account a : accounts) { if (a.id == o.accountid) o.region__c = a.region__c; } } map accbyid = new map (accounts); for (opportunity o : opps) { o.region__c = accbyid.get(o.accountid)?.region__c; } pattern: query parent and children together // one query instead of one per account for (account a : [select id, (select id, email from contacts) from account where id in :ids]) { for (contact c : a.contacts) { /* ... */ } }"},{"id":"checking-limits-at-runtime","h":"Checking limits at runtime","t":"system.debug('soql ' + limits.getqueries() + '/' + limits.getlimitqueries()); system.debug('dml ' + limits.getdmlstatements() + '/' + limits.getlimitdmlstatements()); system.debug('cpu ' + limits.getcputime() + '/' + limits.getlimitcputime() + ' ms'); system.debug('heap ' + limits.getheapsize() + '/' + limits.getlimitheapsize()); // defensive: hand work off before hitting the wall if (limits.getqueries() > limits.getlimitqueries() - 10) { system.enqueuejob(new continueworkjob(remainingids)); return; } every debug log ends with a limit_usage_for_ns block showing usage for the transaction. the apex log analyzer extension for vs code turns logs into flame charts showing where cpu time went."},{"id":"org-wide-limits","h":"Org-wide limits","t":"limit notes api requests per 24 hours based on edition and licences. check setup → company information or get /services/data/vxx.x/limits . async apex executions per 24 hours greater of 250,000 or 200 × user licences scheduled apex jobs 100 at once batch jobs 5 processing concurrently, 100 in the flex queue single emails per day to external addresses edition-based (setup → company information) platform event publishing and delivery hourly/daily allocations by edition and add-ons data and file storage edition + per-licence allocation map lims = orglimits.getmap(); system.orglimit api = lims.get('dailyapirequests'); system.debug(api.getvalue() + ' of ' + api.getlimit() + ' api calls used');"},{"id":"selective-queries-and-indexes","h":"Selective queries and indexes","t":"on large objects (hundreds of thousands of rows or more), the query optimiser only uses an index when the filter is selective . non-selective queries in triggers on big objects fail with non-selective query against large object type . indexed fields standard: id , name , ownerid , createddate , systemmodstamp , recordtypeid , lookup and master-detail fields, email on contacts and leads. custom fields marked external id or unique . custom indexes salesforce support adds on request (including some deterministic formula fields). selectivity thresholds index selective if the filter returns fewer than… standard index 30% of the first million records + 15% of the rest, up to 1,000,000 rows custom index 10% of the first million + 5% of the rest, up to 333,333 rows what defeats an index != , not in , not like , excludes leading wildcards: like '%smith' comparing to null on most fields ( field__c = null ) non-deterministic formula fields or conditions where any branch is non-selective use the query plan tool (developer console → help → preferences → enable query plan) to see the cost and which index would be used. a cost under 1 means selective."},{"id":"large-data-volumes","h":"Large data volumes","t":"archive old records (to big objects, an external store, or salesforce archive) so working tables stay small. skinny tables (via support) hold frequently used fields from an object in one table, avoiding joins. bulk api 2.0 for loads and extracts; pk chunking for very large extracts. defer sharing calculations (via support) during big ownership or role changes. load in parent-first order and sort child csvs by parent id to reduce lock contention."},{"id":"data-skew","h":"Data skew","t":"account (parent) skew more than about 10,000 child records under one parent (e.g. a catch-all \"unknown customer\" account). updating children locks the parent, causing unable_to_lock_row errors and slow sharing recalculation. spread children across several parents. ownership skew one user owning more than about 10,000 records of an object (often an integration user). role or group changes trigger huge sharing recalculations. put such users in a role at the top of the hierarchy or with no role, and spread ownership. lookup skew many records pointing to one lookup target (e.g. every case referencing one \"default\" product). similar locking problems on insert."},{"id":"cpu-time-tips","h":"CPU time tips","t":"exit early: check if anything relevant changed before doing work. use maps and sets, not nested loops or list.contains in loops. reduce describe calls; cache them in statics. let the database filter: add where clauses rather than filtering in apex. consolidate automation: ten record-triggered flows on one object each add overhead. move non-urgent work async (queueable, async flow path, platform events). time spent waiting on the database and callouts doesn't count toward cpu time — apex code, formulas and flow processing do."}]},{"url":"lwc.html","title":"Lightning Web Components","intro":"lightning web components lwc is salesforce's ui framework, built on web standards: custom elements, shadow dom, es modules and templates. if you know modern javascript, most of lwc is familiar; the salesforce parts are data access and where components can be placed.","sections":[{"id":"a-component-is-a-folder","h":"A component is a folder","t":"force-app/main/default/lwc/ └── accountcontacts/ ├── accountcontacts.html # template (required for ui components) ├── accountcontacts.js # class (required) ├── accountcontacts.css # scoped styles (optional) ├── accountcontacts.js-meta.xml # where it can be used (required) └── __tests__/ └── accountcontacts.test.js # jest tests folder and file names are camelcase; in markup they become kebab-case with the c- namespace: <c-account-contacts> . create one with sf lightning generate component --name accountcontacts --type lwc --output-dir force-app/main/default/lwc ."},{"id":"a-complete-example","h":"A complete example","t":"import { lightningelement, api, wire } from 'lwc'; import { refreshapex } from '@salesforce/apex'; import { showtoastevent } from 'lightning/platformshowtoastevent'; import { navigationmixin } from 'lightning/navigation'; import getcontacts from '@salesforce/apex/contactcontroller.getcontacts'; import setprimary from '@salesforce/apex/contactcontroller.setprimary'; const columns = [ { label: 'name', fieldname: 'name' }, { label: 'email', fieldname: 'email', type: 'email' }, { label: 'title', fieldname: 'title' }, { type: 'action', typeattributes: { rowactions: [ { label: 'view', name: 'view' }, { label: 'make primary', name: 'primary' } ] } } ]; export default class accountcontacts extends navigationmixin(lightningelement) { @api recordid; // set automatically on a record page @api maxrows = 25; // configurable in app builder (see meta file) columns = columns; searchterm = ''; wiredresult; // keep the wire result for refreshapex contacts = []; error; // re-runs whenever recordid, searchterm or maxrows changes ('$' = reactive) @wire(getcontacts, { accountid: '$recordid', searchterm: '$searchterm', maxrows: '$maxrows' }) wiredcontacts(result) { this.wiredresult = result; if (result.data) { this.contacts = result.data; this.error = undefined; } else if (result.error) { this.error = result.error; this.contacts = []; } } get hascontacts() { return this.contacts.length > 0; } get errormessage() { return this.error?.body?.message ?? 'unknown error'; } handlesearch(event) { cleartimeout(this.timer); const value = event.target.value; this.timer = settimeout(() => { this.searchterm = value; }, 300); // debounce } async handlerowaction(event) { const { action, row } = event.detail; if (action.name === 'view') { this[navigationmixin.navigate]({ type: 'standard__recordpage', attributes: { recordid: row.id, objectapiname: 'contact', actionname: 'view' } }); } else if (action.name === 'primary') { try { await setprimary({ contactid: row.id, accountid: this.recordid }); // imperative call this.dispatchevent(new showtoastevent({ title: 'saved', message: row.name + ' is now primary', variant: 'success' })); await refreshapex(this.wiredresult); } catch (e) { this.dispatchevent(new showtoastevent({ title: 'error', message: e.body?.message, variant: 'error' })); } } } } {errormessage} no contacts found. public with sharing class contactcontroller { @auraenabled(cacheable=true) // required for @wire; no dml allowed public static list getcontacts(id accountid, string searchterm, integer maxrows) { string term = '%' + (searchterm ?? '') + '%'; return [select id, name, email, title from contact where accountid = :accountid and name like :term with user_mode order by name limit :maxrows]; } @auraenabled // not cacheable: performs dml public static void setprimary(id contactid, id accountid) { try { update as user new account(id = accountid, primary_contact__c = contactid); } catch (exception e) { throw new aurahandledexception(e.getmessage()); // message reaches the component } } } 64.0 true account contacts lightning__recordpage lightning__apppage lightning__homepage account"},{"id":"templates","h":"Templates","t":"syntax does {property} binds a property or getter. no expressions — compute in a getter instead. lwc:if={x} / lwc:elseif={y} / lwc:else conditional rendering (replaces the older if:true / if:false ). for:each={items} for:item=\"item\" loop; each child needs key={item.id} . iterator:it={items} loop with it.first , it.last , it.index , it.value . onclick={handleclick} event handler (lowercase event name prefixed with on ). lwc:ref=\"name\" reference an element as this.refs.name . lwc:spread={props} pass an object of properties to a child. <slot> , <slot name=\"footer\"> content projection from the parent. lwc:dom=\"manual\" allow third-party code to manipulate a container's dom. {opp.name} — {it.value.label} (final)"},{"id":"reactivity-and-decorators","h":"Reactivity and decorators","t":"all class fields are reactive: assigning a new value re-renders. for objects and arrays, assign a new reference ( this.items = [...this.items, x] ) — mutating in place isn't tracked unless the field uses @track . @api : public property or method, set by the parent or the page. treat @api values as read-only inside the component. @wire : reactive data from an adapter or cacheable=true apex. assign to a property ( { data, error } ) or a function. getters recalculate on each render — use them for derived values."},{"id":"lifecycle-hooks","h":"Lifecycle hooks","t":"hook when use for constructor() created (call super() first) rarely needed; can't access attributes or children connectedcallback() inserted into the dom load data imperatively, subscribe to message channels, add listeners renderedcallback() after every render third-party libraries, dom measurements (guard against running repeatedly) disconnectedcallback() removed from the dom unsubscribe, clear timers errorcallback(error, stack) a descendant threw error boundaries"},{"id":"communicating-between-components","h":"Communicating between components","t":"parent → child: @api child → parent: custom events import { lightningelement, api } from 'lwc'; export default class orderline extends lightningelement { @api line; @api currencycode; handleclick() { this.dispatchevent(new customevent('remove', { detail: { lineid: this.line.id } // event names: lowercase, no 'on' prefix // bubbles: true, composed: true // only if it must cross shadow boundaries })); } } unrelated components: lightning message service record selected true recordid import { lightningelement, wire } from 'lwc'; import { publish, subscribe, unsubscribe, messagecontext } from 'lightning/messageservice'; import record_selected from '@salesforce/messagechannel/record_selected__c'; export default class publisher extends lightningelement { @wire(messagecontext) messagecontext; select(id) { publish(this.messagecontext, record_selected, { recordid: id }); } } export class subscriber extends lightningelement { // (separate component in practice) @wire(messagecontext) messagecontext; subscription; connectedcallback() { this.subscription = subscribe(this.messagecontext, record_selected, (msg) => this.load(msg.recordid)); } disconnectedcallback() { unsubscribe(this.subscription); } }"},{"id":"lightning-data-service-no-apex","h":"Lightning Data Service (no Apex)","t":"lds reads and writes records through a shared client cache, respects security, and keeps every component showing the same record in sync. prefer it to apex for single-record work. import { lightningelement, api, wire } from 'lwc'; import { getrecord, getfieldvalue, updaterecord } from 'lightning/uirecordapi'; import name from '@salesforce/schema/account.name'; import rating from '@salesforce/schema/account.rating'; import owner_name from '@salesforce/schema/account.owner.name'; export default class accountrating extends lightningelement { @api recordid; @wire(getrecord, { recordid: '$recordid', fields: [name, rating, owner_name] }) account; get name() { return getfieldvalue(this.account.data, name); } get owner() { return getfieldvalue(this.account.data, owner_name); } async markhot() { await updaterecord({ fields: { id: this.recordid, [rating.fieldapiname]: 'hot' } }); // the wired record refreshes automatically } } need use a whole form with no code lightning-record-form (layout or field list, view/edit modes) a custom form layout lightning-record-edit-form + lightning-input-field ; lightning-record-view-form + lightning-output-field read fields getrecord , getfieldvalue create / update / delete createrecord , updaterecord , deleterecord object and picklist metadata getobjectinfo , getpicklistvalues ( lightning/uiobjectinfoapi ) related lists getrelatedlistrecords ( lightning/uirelatedlistapi ) queries across objects with filters graphql wire adapter, or apex tell lds a record changed outside it (e.g. apex dml) notifyrecordupdateavailable([{ recordid }])"},{"id":"calling-apex-wire-vs-imperative","h":"Calling Apex: wire vs imperative","t":"@wire imperative apex annotation @auraenabled(cacheable=true) @auraenabled (cacheable optional) when it runs automatically, and again when reactive params change when your code calls it (returns a promise) dml allowed no yes (if not cacheable) refresh refreshapex(wiredresult) call it again use for reading data for display saving, button clicks, conditional loads apex parameters are passed as an object with matching names; return types can be sobjects, lists, maps, primitives or classes with @auraenabled properties."},{"id":"other-platform-modules","h":"Other platform modules","t":"import user_id from '@salesforce/user/id'; import is_guest from '@salesforce/user/isguest'; import greeting from '@salesforce/label/c.greeting'; // custom label import can_approve from '@salesforce/custompermission/approve_discounts'; import logo from '@salesforce/resourceurl/companylogo'; // static resource import { loadscript, loadstyle } from 'lightning/platformresourceloader'; import { closeactionscreenevent } from 'lightning/actions'; // close a quick action import { flownavigationnextevent, flowattributechangeevent } from 'lightning/flowsupport'; import { getobjectinfo } from 'lightning/uiobjectinfoapi'; import { subscribe as empsubscribe } from 'lightning/empapi'; // platform events / cdc in the ui"},{"id":"modals","h":"Modals","t":"import { api } from 'lwc'; import lightningmodal from 'lightning/modal'; export default class confirmmodal extends lightningmodal { @api message; handleok() { this.close('ok'); } handlecancel() { this.close('cancel'); } } {message} import confirmmodal from 'c/confirmmodal'; async handledelete() { const result = await confirmmodal.open({ size: 'small', message: 'delete this record?' }); if (result === 'ok') { /* delete */ } }"},{"id":"where-components-can-go","h":"Where components can go","t":"target place lightning__recordpage , lightning__apppage , lightning__homepage lightning app builder pages lightning__recordaction quick actions (screen or headless with actiontype ) lightning__flowscreen custom screen-flow components lightning__utilitybar the app utility bar lightning__tab a custom lightning component tab lightningcommunity__page , lightningcommunity__default experience builder sites import { lightningelement, api } from 'lwc'; import { flowattributechangeevent } from 'lightning/flowsupport'; export default class starrating extends lightningelement { @api label; // exposed as a flow input in the meta file @api value = 0; // exposed as input and output handleclick(event) { const v = number(event.currenttarget.dataset.value); this.dispatchevent(new flowattributechangeevent('value', v)); // tell flow } @api validate() { // called by flow on next return this.value > 0 ? { isvalid: true } : { isvalid: false, errormessage: 'pick a rating' }; } }"},{"id":"styling","h":"Styling","t":"component css is scoped by shadow dom: it can't leak out, and outside css can't reach in. use the salesforce lightning design system (slds) utility classes ( slds-p-around_medium , slds-grid , slds-text-heading_small ) and base components ( lightning-* ) so your ui matches the platform. style base components with styling hooks (css custom properties) such as --slds-c-button-brand-color-background , not by targeting their internals."},{"id":"testing-with-jest","h":"Testing with Jest","t":"import { createelement } from 'lwc'; import accountrating from 'c/accountrating'; import { getrecord } from 'lightning/uirecordapi'; const mockrecord = { fields: { name: { value: 'acme' }, rating: { value: 'warm' }, owner: { value: { fields: { name: { value: 'ada' } } } } } }; describe('c-account-rating', () => { aftereach(() => { while (document.body.firstchild) document.body.removechild(document.body.firstchild); }); it('shows the account name from lds', async () => { const el = createelement('c-account-rating', { is: accountrating }); el.recordid = '001000000000001aaa'; document.body.appendchild(el); getrecord.emit(mockrecord); // push mock data through the wire adapter await promise.resolve(); // let the dom re-render expect(el.shadowroot.textcontent).tocontain('acme'); }); }); npm install --save-dev @salesforce/sfdx-lwc-jest npx sfdx-lwc-jest # run all tests npx sfdx-lwc-jest --watch"},{"id":"debugging","h":"Debugging","t":"setup → debug mode: enable for your user to get unminified framework code and clearer errors (slower; turn it off afterwards). browser devtools: components appear as custom elements; use console.log(json.stringify(proxy)) to see reactive proxies' values. apex errors reach the component as error.body.message ; throw aurahandledexception for user-friendly messages. lightning locker / lightning web security isolate components by namespace; some global apis are restricted. aura and visualforce are the older frameworks. you'll maintain them in existing orgs, and lwcs can be nested inside aura components (not the reverse). build new ui in lwc."}]},{"url":"order-of-execution.html","title":"Order of execution","intro":"order of execution when a record is saved, salesforce runs a fixed sequence of steps. knowing it explains \"why did my value get overwritten?\" and \"why did this run twice?\"","sections":[{"id":"the-sequence","h":"The sequence","t":"simplified from the official list; highlighted steps are the ones you'll work with most. load the original record from the database (or initialise a new one) and apply the new field values. from the ui, the browser has already checked dependent picklists and field lengths. system validation (ui saves): required fields on the layout, field formats, max lengths. api and apex saves only check things like foreign keys here. before-save record-triggered flows (fast field updates) before triggers (apex) system validation again (now for all saves: required fields at field level, formats), then custom validation rules . because validation runs after before-triggers, a trigger can fix a value before it's validated. duplicate rules . a blocking rule stops the save here. record is saved to the database but not committed . the id now exists. after triggers (apex) assignment rules (leads, cases) auto-response rules workflow rules (legacy). if a workflow field update fires, the record is updated again and before and after update triggers run one more time (custom validation rules do not). escalation rules processes (legacy process builder) and flows they launch after-save record-triggered flows entitlement rules roll-up summary fields on the parent are recalculated → the parent goes through its own save procedure. same for grandparents. criteria-based sharing evaluated commit : all dml in the transaction becomes permanent. post-commit : emails are sent, async apex (queueable, future) starts, platform events published \"after commit\" are delivered, asynchronous flow paths run. full official list in the apex developer guide →"},{"id":"one-transaction-one-set-of-limits","h":"One transaction, one set of limits","t":"everything from step 1 to the commit is one transaction : an unhandled exception anywhere — a trigger, a flow fault, a validation rule failing on a child record you updated — rolls back every change in the transaction. all triggers, flows and roll-ups share one set of governor limits . a flow that does 30 queries and a trigger that does 80 together exceed the 100-query limit. dml inside a trigger starts the whole sequence again for the other object, nested inside the same transaction. sequencediagram participant u as user saves opportunity participant o as opportunity automation participant a as account automation u->>o: before flows, before triggers, validation o->>o: save (uncommitted) o->>o: after triggers → update account o->>a: account: before → validation → save → after a-->>o: back to opportunity o->>o: after-save flows o->>a: roll-up recalculates → account saves again o->>u: commit (or roll back everything)"},{"id":"bulk-saves","h":"Bulk saves","t":"when many records are saved together (api, data loader, a flow updating a collection), salesforce processes them in chunks of 200 . each step runs once per chunk with all 200 records: the before trigger sees trigger.new with 200 records, then validation runs for all 200, and so on. that's why bulkified code matters: one query per chunk, not one per record. with allornone = false (partial success), records that fail validation are removed and the remaining ones are re-run through the triggers, so triggers can execute more than once for the same records (up to three attempts)."},{"id":"explaining-common-surprises","h":"Explaining common surprises","t":"symptom explanation a value set in a before-save flow is overwritten a before trigger runs after before-save flows and changed it. a trigger fires twice for one save a legacy workflow field update re-runs update triggers; or a roll-up on a parent updates the parent, whose trigger updates the child again. add recursion control ( triggers ). a validation rule didn't block a value set by automation values set by legacy workflow field updates re-run triggers but not custom validation rules. values set in a before trigger are validated, because validation runs after it. a callout fails with \"uncommitted work pending\" dml already happened in this transaction. move the callout to a queueable or an async flow path (post-commit). an email was sent even though the save failed it wasn't: emails go out post-commit. if you saw one, it came from a separate, successful transaction (e.g. async path or a retried save). platform event received for a record that doesn't exist the event was published \"immediately\" and the transaction later rolled back. use \"publish after commit\" for events about saved data. changing a child record changes the parent's lastmodifieddate a roll-up summary on the parent recalculated and saved the parent."},{"id":"what-doesn-t-follow-this-sequence","h":"What doesn't follow this sequence","t":"some operations skip triggers : cascade-deleting master-detail children, mass address updates, renaming picklist values, and a few others don't fire triggers on the affected records. lead conversion fires triggers on the lead and on the created account, contact and opportunity — but not validation rules on the lead unless enabled in lead settings. merge fires delete triggers on the losing records and update triggers on the winner. undelete fires after undelete triggers only."}]},{"url":"reports.html","title":"Reports & dashboards","intro":"reports & dashboards reports are saved queries with grouping and formatting; dashboards are charts built on reports. both respect the viewer's security.","sections":[{"id":"report-formats","h":"Report formats","t":"format shape use for tabular a flat list of rows exports, call lists, simple lists. can't feed most dashboard charts unless row-limited. summary rows grouped by up to 3 fields, with subtotals \"pipeline by stage\", \"cases by owner\" matrix grouped by rows and columns \"opportunity amount by owner × quarter\" joined several blocks, each from its own report type, sharing a grouping \"opportunities and cases per account\" side by side the report builder only shows formats implicitly: add a row grouping and it becomes summary; add a column grouping and it becomes matrix."},{"id":"report-types","h":"Report types","t":"a report type decides which objects a report can use and which records appear. standard report types exist for most objects. create custom report types (setup → report types) when you need custom objects, specific relationships or \"without\" logic. relationship choice returns example a with b only a records that have at least one b (inner join) accounts with opportunities → no accounts without deals a with or without b all a records, plus b where it exists (outer join) all accounts, with their opportunities if any report types can go up to four objects deep (a → b → c → d). add fields via lookup (\"add fields related via lookup\") to bring in parent fields without adding a level. \"i can't find my field in the report\" almost always means the report type's field layout doesn't include it, or the user lacks field-level security. edit the custom report type's layout to add new fields."},{"id":"filters","h":"Filters","t":"standard filters : show me (my / my team's / all), date field and range (e.g. close date = current fq). field filters with operators: equals, not equal, less than, contains, starts with, includes (multi-select), and relative dates like last 90 days , next quarter . filter logic : (1 and 2) or 3 . cross filters : \"accounts with / without cases\", optionally with sub-filters (\"without cases where status = open\"). the classic answer to \"show me accounts with no activity\". row limit filters : \"top 10 accounts by revenue\". field-to-field filters: compare two fields (e.g. close date < created date) in the filter panel."},{"id":"calculated-values","h":"Calculated values","t":"bucket fields group values into categories without creating a field: annual revenue into small (< 1m), mid (1m–50m), large (> 50m); or industries into sectors. up to 5 buckets per report. row-level formulas one per report; calculated per row. close_date - datevalue(created_date) summary formulas calculated on grouped totals. use parentgroupval for percent-of-total and prevgroupval for period-over-period. won:sum / closed:sum amount:sum / parentgroupval(amount:sum, grand_summary) (amount:sum - prevgroupval(amount:sum, close_date)) / prevgroupval(amount:sum, close_date)"},{"id":"useful-report-recipes","h":"Useful report recipes","t":"question report which accounts have no open opportunities? accounts report, cross filter accounts without opportunities where closed = false pipeline by stage this quarter opportunities, close date = current fq, isclosed = false, group by stage, sum amount; funnel chart case backlog by age cases, status ≠ closed, bucket on age (0–2, 3–7, 8–30, 30+ days), group by owner leads not touched in 30 days leads, last activity < last 30 days or blank, isconverted = false field changes over time \"opportunity history\" or \"opportunity field history\" report type trend of a metric (e.g. weekly open cases) reporting snapshot : a scheduled job copies report results into a custom object, which you then report on"},{"id":"dashboards","h":"Dashboards","t":"each component (chart, gauge, metric, table) is driven by one source report (summary or matrix, or tabular with a row limit). up to 25 components per dashboard; dashboard filters (up to 5) apply to all components that share the field. running user : a dashboard runs as a specified user (everyone sees that user's data) or as the logged-in user (a dynamic dashboard — each viewer sees their own data). dynamic dashboards are limited per edition. dashboards refresh on demand or on a schedule; data isn't live between refreshes. embed a dashboard on a home or record page with the dashboard component, or a single report chart on a record page with the report chart component (filtered to the record)."},{"id":"sharing-and-subscriptions","h":"Sharing and subscriptions","t":"reports and dashboards live in folders . share folders with users, groups or roles as viewer, editor or manager. private reports live in \"private reports\". subscribe to a report or dashboard to get it by email on a schedule, optionally only when conditions are met (e.g. open p1 cases > 0). report results always respect the viewer's record and field access, except dashboards running as a fixed user."},{"id":"reports-vs-soql","h":"Reports vs SOQL","t":"reports are for people; soql is for code. equivalent logic for the pipeline report: select stagename, count(id) deals, sum(amount) pipeline from opportunity where isclosed = false and closedate = this_fiscal_quarter group by stagename order by sum(amount) desc developers can run a report from apex with the reports namespace ( reports.reportmanager.runreport(reportid, true) ), and from outside through the analytics rest api ( /services/data/vxx.x/analytics/reports/{id} ). for analytics beyond these limits (millions of rows, data from other systems, advanced visualisation), look at crm analytics or tableau ( catalog )."}]},{"url":"resources.html","title":"Docs & certifications","intro":"docs & certifications primary sources first. when this guide and the official documentation disagree, trust the documentation.","sections":[{"id":"suggested-reading-order","h":"Suggested reading order","t":"everyone platform fundamentals data model security & sharing flow order of execution admins, then formulas & validation rules admin essentials reports & dashboards sales & service processes which one do i pick? developers, then apex language soql & sosl triggers governor limits testing apex async apex lwc apis and events devops"},{"id":"official-documentation","h":"Official documentation","t":"resource covers salesforce help admin and feature documentation, knowledge articles apex developer guide language, triggers, async, testing, security apex reference guide every class and method soql and sosl reference every clause and function lwc developer guide components, data, testing component library all lightning-* base components with examples lightning design system slds classes, design guidelines, icons rest api developer guide resources, composite, errors bulk api 2.0 developer guide ingest and query jobs pub/sub api grpc event publishing and subscription platform events developer guide events, triggers, allocations sf cli command reference every command and flag salesforce limits quick reference every limit in one place metadata api developer guide metadata types and their xml salesforce architects well-architected framework, decision guides, diagrams release notes what changed this release salesforce trust status incidents and maintenance by instance"},{"id":"learning","h":"Learning","t":"trailhead : free modules and projects with hands-on challenges checked against a practice org. search for the trails that match the chapter you're reading. developer edition signup : a free, permanent org. salesforce developers blog and salesforce admins : feature explanations from product teams. trailheadapps on github : complete sample apps (lwc recipes, apex recipes, dreamhouse, ebikes) showing current best practice. trailblazer community and salesforce stack exchange : questions and answers."},{"id":"certification-paths","h":"Certification paths","t":"credential for focus salesforce associate anyone new platform basics; optional starting point platform administrator admins configuration, security, data, automation, reports advanced administrator experienced admins complex security, automation, change management platform app builder admins and developers declarative app design, data model, ui platform developer i developers apex, soql, triggers, testing, lwc basics, limits platform developer ii experienced developers advanced apex, performance, integration, testing javascript developer i front-end developers javascript + lwc sales cloud / service cloud consultant consultants and senior admins designing those clouds' processes agentforce specialist admins and developers building ai agents, prompts, trust layer, data 360 grounding data cloud (data 360) consultant data specialists ingestion, modelling, identity resolution, segments architect credentials → certified technical architect (cta) architects domain exams (data, integration, sharing and identity, development lifecycle) leading to the cta review board credential names change occasionally — check the trailhead credentials pages for current names, exam guides and maintenance requirements (most certifications need a short maintenance module each year)."},{"id":"tools-worth-installing","h":"Tools worth installing","t":"vs code + salesforce extension pack (expanded) : apex language server, lwc support, soql builder, org browser, debugger. salesforce cli ( sf ). apex log analyzer (vs code extension): flame charts and timing for debug logs. salesforce code analyzer : static analysis (pmd, eslint and more) — sf plugins install code-analyzer . salesforce inspector reloaded (browser extension): quick soql, field api names, data export from any page. data loader ."}]},{"url":"sales-service.html","title":"Sales & Service processes","intro":"sales & service processes the standard objects encode a sales process and a support process. knowing the intended flow helps you configure it, and explains a lot of built-in behaviour.","sections":[{"id":"the-sales-lifecycle","h":"The sales lifecycle","t":"flowchart lr c[campaign] --> l[lead] w[web-to-lead / import / api] --> l l -- \"qualify & convert\" --> a[account] l --> ct[contact] l --> o[opportunity] o --> p[\"products (line items)\"] o --> q[quote] o -- \"closed won\" --> co[\"contract / order / onboarding\"] leads sources: web forms ( web-to-lead generates an html form that posts into salesforce), imports, marketing automation, api. status tracks qualification (open → working → qualified / unqualified). one status is flagged as the \"converted\" status. lead assignment rules route new leads to owners or queues. lead conversion converting a lead creates (or matches to existing) an account and contact , and optionally an opportunity . the lead becomes read-only with isconverted = true . map custom lead fields to account, contact and opportunity fields in object manager → lead → fields → map lead fields . unmapped data is left behind on the converted lead. campaign history and activities move to the contact. in apex, use database.convertlead : leadstatus converted = [select apiname from leadstatus where isconverted = true limit 1]; database.leadconvert lc = new database.leadconvert(); lc.setleadid(leadid); lc.setconvertedstatus(converted.apiname); lc.setdonotcreateopportunity(false); lc.setopportunityname('acme - new business'); // lc.setaccountid(existingaccountid); // merge into an existing account instead database.leadconvertresult r = database.convertlead(lc); system.debug(r.getaccountid() + ' / ' + r.getcontactid() + ' / ' + r.getopportunityid()); opportunities stages are picklist values, each with a default probability and forecast category (pipeline, best case, commit, closed, omitted). edit them in object manager → opportunity → fields → stage. sales processes choose which stages each record type uses. isclosed and iswon are derived from the stage's type (open / closed-won / closed-lost). opportunity contact roles record who's involved (decision maker, influencer…) and are the link between contacts and deals for campaign influence. opportunity teams and splits share credit across reps. path + kanban list views are the standard way reps move deals through stages. products, price books and quotes create product2 records (the catalogue). add each product to the standard price book (required first) with a list price. create custom price books (e.g. \"emea 2027\", \"partners\") with their own pricebookentry prices. on an opportunity, pick one price book, then add opportunity products ( opportunitylineitem ) with quantity and sales price. the opportunity's amount becomes the sum of line totals. quotes copy the line items, can be synced back to the opportunity, and generate pdfs from templates. complex configuration, discounting and approvals call for cpq / revenue cloud. id stdpb = test.isrunningtest() ? test.getstandardpricebookid() : [select id from pricebook2 where isstandard = true].id; product2 p = new product2(name = 'widget', isactive = true); insert p; pricebookentry pbe = new pricebookentry(pricebook2id = stdpb, product2id = p.id, unitprice = 100, isactive = true); insert pbe; opportunity o = new opportunity(name = 'deal', stagename = 'prospecting', closedate = date.today(), pricebook2id = stdpb); insert o; insert new opportunitylineitem(opportunityid = o.id, pricebookentryid = pbe.id, quantity = 5, unitprice = 90); forecasting and territories collaborative forecasts roll up opportunity amounts (or quantities, or splits) by forecast category, period and the forecast hierarchy (usually the role hierarchy). managers can adjust subordinates' numbers. enterprise territory management assigns accounts to territories by rules (geography, industry, size) independently of the role hierarchy, and grants access accordingly."},{"id":"the-service-lifecycle","h":"The service lifecycle","t":"flowchart lr e[email] --> cs[case] wf[web form] --> cs ch[\"chat / messaging\"] --> cs ph[phone] --> cs cs -- \"assignment rules\" --> q[queue] q -- \"omni-channel routing\" --> ag[agent] ag -- \"knowledge, macros\" --> res[resolve] cs -. \"milestones / escalation\" .-> esc[escalate] res --> cl[closed] cases origin records the channel; status values are flagged open or closed; priority drives routing and slas. support processes pick which statuses each case record type uses. case feed shows emails, posts, calls and field changes in one timeline. parent cases group duplicates or related issues ( parentid ). channels into cases channel how email-to-case customers email support@acme.com ; a forwarding rule sends mail to a salesforce routing address, which creates a case and threads replies by a reference token. web-to-case a generated html form posts cases from your website. messaging (in-app/web, sms, whatsapp) messaging sessions routed through omni-channel, optionally starting with a bot or agentforce agent. service cloud voice telephony integrated into the console, with transcription. experience cloud help centre customers search knowledge and log cases themselves. routing and slas case assignment rules evaluate criteria on new cases and assign to a user or queue. auto-response rules send the customer an acknowledgement email using a template chosen by criteria. escalation rules reassign or notify when a case stays open past a time limit, respecting business hours. omni-channel pushes work to agents based on their presence status and capacity . queue-based routing sends from queues; skills-based routing matches case attributes (language, product) to agent skills. entitlements & milestones an entitlement says what support a customer gets; an entitlement process defines milestones (first response within 1 hour, resolution within 1 business day) with warning and violation actions. business hours & holidays used by escalation rules, milestones and businesshours.diff() in apex. agent productivity service console : a tabbed lightning app showing a case, its contact and account side by side, with a utility bar. knowledge : articles with versions, publication status, data categories for visibility, and channels (internal, customer, partner, public). attach articles to cases. macros run several actions with one click (send templated email, update status, post to feed). quick text : reusable snippets inserted into emails and chats. case merge combines duplicates."},{"id":"activities-and-email","h":"Activities and email","t":"tasks (to-dos, logged calls, emails) and events (calendar) relate to a person ( whoid : lead or contact) and a thing ( whatid : account, opportunity, case, custom object). with shared activities , one activity can relate to up to 50 contacts (stored in taskrelation / eventrelation ). einstein activity capture syncs email and calendar from gmail/outlook; captured items aren't always stored as standard activity records, which affects reporting."}]},{"url":"security.html","title":"Security & sharing","intro":"security & sharing access is decided in layers. a user must pass every layer to see a value: the org, the object, the field, and finally the specific record.","sections":[{"id":"the-layers","h":"The layers","t":"flowchart td a[\"1 · org access login, mfa/sso, ip ranges, login hours, session settings\"] --> b[\"2 · object access read / create / edit / delete / view all / modify all\"] b --> c[\"3 · field access field-level security: read, edit\"] c --> d[\"4 · record access\"] d --> e[\"org-wide defaults: the baseline\"] e --> f[\"+ role hierarchy\"] f --> g[\"+ sharing rules, teams, manual & apex sharing\"] g --> h[\"− restriction rules filter what's visible\"] question controlled by can they log in, from where, when? profile (login hours, ip ranges), session settings, mfa, sso, network access can they use this object at all? object permissions in permission sets / profile can they see/edit this field? field-level security in permission sets / profile which records can they see? org-wide defaults + role hierarchy + sharing can they do this system action (export, run reports, api)? system permissions in permission sets / profile which fields show on the page? page layout / dynamic forms (ui only — not security)"},{"id":"profiles-permission-sets-and-groups","h":"Profiles, permission sets and groups","t":"profile exactly one per user. holds login hours and ip ranges, default record types, page layout assignments, default app, and (historically) object, field and system permissions. best practice now: give everyone a minimal profile and grant access with permission sets. permission set a bundle of object, field, tab, app, apex class, visualforce page, custom permission and system permissions. a user can have many. permissions only add access; nothing in salesforce's permission model takes access away. permission set group a bundle of permission sets assigned as one — a persona like \"support agent\". a muting permission set inside the group can switch off specific permissions that the included sets would otherwise grant. custom permission a named on/off flag you create (e.g. bypass_validation , approve_discounts ) and grant through permission sets. check it in formulas ( $permission.approve_discounts ), flow, apex ( featuremanagement.checkpermission('approve_discounts') ) and lwc ( @salesforce/custompermission/approve_discounts ). permission set licence unlocks add-on features (e.g. cpq, field service). must be assigned before the permission set that uses it. object permissions permission grants read see records the user has record-level access to create / edit / delete those actions, still subject to record access view all read every record of the object, ignoring sharing modify all read, edit, delete, transfer every record, ignoring sharing view all fields read every field on the object regardless of fls system permissions view all data and modify all data do the same across every object. reserve them for admins and a few integration users."},{"id":"org-wide-defaults-owd","h":"Org-wide defaults (OWD)","t":"setup → sharing settings. the owd is the baseline record access for users who don't own the record. set it to what the most restricted user should have, then open up with the tools below. setting non-owners can… typical use private not see records (unless shared) opportunities, sensitive custom objects public read only see, not edit accounts in many orgs, reference data public read/write see and edit collaborative objects with no sensitivity public read/write/transfer see, edit, change owner leads and cases only controlled by parent inherit from the parent record contacts (from account); all master-detail children public full access everything campaigns only there are separate internal and external defaults; external (experience cloud users) can be the same or more restrictive. grant access using hierarchies is always on for standard objects and can be switched off for custom objects."},{"id":"role-hierarchy","h":"Role hierarchy","t":"users above someone in the role hierarchy get the same access to that person's records (and, by default, to records shared with them). roles model data visibility , not the org chart — keep the hierarchy as flat as your visibility needs allow, since deep hierarchies slow down sharing recalculation. flowchart td ceo --> vps[\"vp sales\"] ceo --> vpsv[\"vp service\"] vps --> nam[\"sales manager, east\"] vps --> wam[\"sales manager, west\"] nam --> r1[\"sales rep, east\"] wam --> r2[\"sales rep, west\"] vpsv --> sup[\"support manager\"] --> ag[\"support agent\"] here the east manager sees east reps' private opportunities; west reps don't. vp sales sees all sales records; vp service sees none of them unless sharing rules open them up."},{"id":"sharing-rules-and-other-ways-to-share","h":"Sharing rules and other ways to share","t":"mechanism how it works example owner-based sharing rule records owned by members of a group/role → shared with another group/role opportunities owned by east sales → read only to west sales criteria-based sharing rule records matching field criteria → shared with a group/role accounts where region__c = 'emea' → read/write to emea team guest user sharing rule share records with unauthenticated experience cloud visitors published faqs account / opportunity / case teams named people get access to one record with a role solution engineer added to an opportunity team manual sharing owner (or above) clicks sharing on one record one-off access enterprise territory management account access by territory assignment rules accounts in california → west territory users apex managed sharing code inserts rows into the __share table share an invoice with the approver chosen on the record implicit sharing automatic, built in owner of a contact/opportunity/case can read its parent account public groups are the building block for most sharing rules: a named set of users, roles, roles-and-subordinates and other groups. implicit sharing parent implicit : if you can see a contact, opportunity or case, you get read access to its account. child implicit : the account owner gets access to the account's child records, at the level set on the owner's role (\"opportunity access\", \"case access\", \"contact access\"). master-detail children always inherit from the master."},{"id":"restriction-and-scoping-rules","h":"Restriction and scoping rules","t":"restriction rules work in the opposite direction from sharing: even if sharing grants access, the user only sees records matching the rule's criteria. example: contractors see only tasks where type = 'contractor' . available on custom objects, contracts, events, tasks, time sheets and a few others. scoping rules set a default filter on what users see in list views, search and lookups (e.g. \"my region's accounts\") without removing access."},{"id":"security-in-apex","h":"Security in Apex","t":"apex runs in system mode by default: it ignores object permissions, fls and (unless told otherwise) sharing. you choose how much security to enforce. tool enforces example with sharing record sharing for the running user public with sharing class accountservice without sharing explicitly ignores sharing utility that must see all records inherited sharing uses the caller's mode (defaults to with sharing at an entry point) reusable selector classes with user_mode sharing + object + field permissions on a query [select name from account with user_mode] insert as user / accesslevel.user_mode same, for dml database.update(recs, accesslevel.user_mode) security.stripinaccessible removes fields the user can't access instead of throwing sanitise records before returning them to a ui schema...isaccessible() manual checks account.sobjecttype.getdescribe().isupdateable() public with sharing class contactcontroller { @auraenabled(cacheable=true) public static list recentcontacts() { // throws a queryexception if the user lacks access to contact or any field return [select id, name, email from contact with user_mode order by createddate desc limit 20]; } @auraenabled public static void save(list contacts) { // dml in user mode: respects crud, fls and sharing update as user contacts; } public static list forexport() { list accs = [select id, name, annualrevenue, rating from account limit 100]; // silently drop fields the running user can't read sobjectaccessdecision d = security.stripinaccessible(accesstype.readable, accs); system.debug('removed: ' + d.getremovedfields()); return (list ) d.getrecords(); } } apex managed sharing for custom objects, define an apex sharing reason (object manager → the object → apex sharing reasons) so your shares survive owner changes and are identifiable. then insert share rows. public without sharing class invoicesharing { public static void sharewithapprovers(list invoices) { list shares = new list (); for (invoice__c inv : invoices) { if (inv.approver__c == null || inv.approver__c == inv.ownerid) continue; shares.add(new invoice__share( parentid = inv.id, userorgroupid = inv.approver__c, accesslevel = 'edit', // 'read' or 'edit' rowcause = schema.invoice__share.rowcause.approver__c // the apex sharing reason )); } // allornone = false: a share for an inactive user shouldn't fail the rest database.saveresult[] results = database.insert(shares, false); for (database.saveresult r : results) { if (!r.issuccess()) system.debug(logginglevel.warn, r.geterrors()[0].getmessage()); } } } standard objects use their own share objects ( accountshare , opportunityshare , caseshare ) with object-specific access fields, and only the manual row cause."},{"id":"debugging-i-can-t-see-it","h":"Debugging \"I can't see it\"","t":"is the field on the page? page layout or dynamic forms visibility rule. not security, but the most common cause. licence : does the user's licence include the object? object access : setup → users → the user → view summary (on the permission set assignments) shows the combined effect of profile + permission sets for each object and field. field-level security : same summary, or object manager → field → set field-level security . record access : on the record, sharing → view sharing hierarchy shows who has access and why. or query userrecordaccess : select recordid, hasreadaccess, haseditaccess, hasdeleteaccess, maxaccesslevel from userrecordaccess where userid = '005xxxxxxxxxxxx' and recordid = '001xxxxxxxxxxxx' select parent.label, parent.isownedbyprofile, permissionsread, permissionsedit, permissionsviewallrecords from objectpermissions where sobjecttype = 'invoice__c' and parentid in (select permissionsetid from permissionsetassignment where assigneeid = '005xxxxxxxxxxxx') restriction rules filtering it out? test as them : setup → users → login (requires \"administrators can log in as any user\" in login access policies)."},{"id":"org-level-security","h":"Org-level security","t":"multi-factor authentication is required for ui logins. sso via saml or openid connect can satisfy it. session settings : timeout, lock sessions to ip, require httponly, clickjack protection. network access : trusted ip ranges (skip identity verification) vs login ip ranges on the profile (block logins from elsewhere). health check (setup → health check) scores settings against salesforce's baseline. setup audit trail , login history , field history tracking , and (with shield) event monitoring and field audit trail give you an audit record. shield platform encryption encrypts data at rest at the field and file level with tenant-managed keys."}]},{"url":"soql.html","title":"SOQL & SOSL","intro":"soql & sosl soql (salesforce object query language) reads records from one object and its relationships. sosl (salesforce object search language) runs full-text search across many objects. neither supports arbitrary joins or select * .","sections":[{"id":"where-you-run-queries","h":"Where you run queries","t":"apex : inline in square brackets, [select … ] , or dynamically with database.query . developer console → query editor (tick \"use tooling api\" for metadata like apexclass). cli : sf data query --query \"select id, name from account limit 5\" (add --result-format csv or --bulk ). rest api : get /services/data/v64.0/query?q=select+id+from+account . vs code's soql builder, workbench and other tools."},{"id":"syntax","h":"Syntax","t":"select fieldlist [, (subquery)] from objecttype [where conditionexpression] [with user_mode | system_mode | security_enforced] [group by fieldlist [having condition]] [order by field [asc|desc] [nulls first|last]] [limit n] [offset n] [for update | for view | for reference] [all rows] -- apex only: include deleted and archived rows"},{"id":"filtering","h":"Filtering","t":"select id, name, annualrevenue from account where industry = 'technology' -- strings in single quotes and annualrevenue >= 1000000 -- numbers unquoted and billingcountry in ('us', 'ca') and name like 'acme%' -- % any chars, _ one char; case-insensitive and rating != null and (type = 'customer' or type = 'partner') and not name like '%test%' and industry not in ('government') and isdeleted = false and createddate > 2026-01-01t00:00:00z -- datetimes in iso 8601 utc and last_review__c operator notes = != < <= > >= = null and != null work (no is null ) like text only; % and _ wildcards; leading % can't use indexes in , not in literal list, bind variable, or subquery (semi-join / anti-join) includes , excludes multi-select picklists: interests__c includes ('golf;tennis', 'sailing') means (golf and tennis) or sailing distance(…) < n geolocation: distance(location__c, geolocation(37.77,-122.41), 'mi') < 20"},{"id":"relationship-queries","h":"Relationship queries","t":"child to parent (dot notation) select id, lastname, account.name, account.owner.name, account.parent.name from contact where account.industry = 'healthcare' and account.owner.isactive = true -- custom lookup: use __r select id, name, invoice__r.status__c, invoice__r.account__r.name from invoice_line__c up to 5 levels up ( a.b.c.d.e.field ), and up to 55 child-to-parent relationships in one query. parent to children (subquery) select id, name, (select id, lastname, email from contacts order by lastname), (select id, amount, stagename from opportunities where isclosed = false), (select id from invoices__r) -- custom child relationship from account where id in :accountids use the child relationship name (plural: contacts , opportunities , cases , invoices__r ). up to 20 subqueries per query; nesting up to 5 levels deep in recent api versions. for (account a : [select name, (select lastname from contacts) from account limit 10]) { system.debug(a.name + ' has ' + a.contacts.size() + ' contacts'); for (contact c : a.contacts) system.debug(' ' + c.lastname); } semi-joins and anti-joins -- accounts with at least one open case select id, name from account where id in (select accountid from case where isclosed = false) -- accounts with no opportunities at all select id, name from account where id not in (select accountid from opportunity) -- contacts at accounts that have a won deal this year select id, name from contact where accountid in (select accountid from opportunity where iswon = true and closedate = this_year)"},{"id":"aggregates","h":"Aggregates","t":"select stagename, count(id) deals, sum(amount) total, avg(amount) avgdeal, max(closedate) latest from opportunity where closedate = this_fiscal_year group by stagename having sum(amount) > 100000 order by sum(amount) desc -- distinct count select count_distinct(accountid) from opportunity where iswon = true -- subtotals select leadsource, rating, count(id) from lead group by rollup(leadsource, rating) -- find duplicates select email, count(id) n from contact where email != null group by email having count(id) > 1 for (aggregateresult ar : [select ownerid, sum(amount) amt from opportunity where isclosed = false group by ownerid]) { id owner = (id) ar.get('ownerid'); decimal amt = (decimal) ar.get('amt'); // alias; unaliased are expr0, expr1… } integer opencases = [select count() from case where isclosed = false]; aggregate queries still count every row they scan toward the 50,000-row limit, and return at most 2,000 groups."},{"id":"dates","h":"Dates","t":"literal meaning today , yesterday , tomorrow calendar days in the user's time zone this_week , last_week , next_week weeks (start day depends on locale) this_month , last_month , next_month months this_quarter , this_year , last_year calendar quarters/years this_fiscal_quarter , last_fiscal_year fiscal periods from org settings last_n_days:30 , next_n_days:7 rolling ranges (last_n_days includes today) n_days_ago:90 , last_n_months:6 , next_n_quarters:2 more rolling forms select id from case where createddate = last_n_days:7 select id from opportunity where closedate = next_quarter select id from task where activitydate"},{"id":"other-clauses-and-functions","h":"Other clauses and functions","t":"-- all fields (fields(all) and fields(custom) need limit 200 or less) select fields(standard) from account limit 10 -- picklist labels and formatted values select tolabel(stagename), format(amount), convertcurrency(amount) from opportunity -- polymorphic fields: choose fields per type select id, subject, typeof what when account then name, industry when opportunity then name, amount else name end from task -- filter on polymorphic type select id from task where what.type = 'opportunity' select id from case where owner.type = 'queue' -- lock rows for update within this transaction (apex) select id, balance__c from wallet__c where id = :walletid for update -- include the recycle bin (apex) select id, name from account where isdeleted = true all rows -- paging (offset max 2,000) select id, name from account order by name limit 50 offset 100"},{"id":"soql-in-apex","h":"SOQL in Apex","t":"// bind variables (safe from injection) set ids = trigger.newmap.keyset(); string industry = 'technology'; list accs = [select id, name from account where id in :ids and industry = :industry]; // bind expressions list recent = [select id from contact where createddate > :datetime.now().addhours(-1)]; // single record: throws queryexception if 0 rows (or more than 1) account one = [select id from account where name = 'acme' limit 1]; // safer single record list found = [select id from account where name = 'acme' limit 1]; account maybe = found.isempty() ? null : found[0]; // map by id map byid = new map ([select id, email from contact where accountid in :ids]); // soql for-loop: fetches 200 at a time — low heap for large result sets for (list chunk : [select id, email from contact where email = null]) { // process up to 200 records per iteration } // enforce the user's permissions list visible = [select id, name from account with user_mode];"},{"id":"dynamic-soql","h":"Dynamic SOQL","t":"// build queries at runtime — e.g. configurable fields or filters list fields = new list { 'id', 'name', 'industry' }; string soql = 'select ' + string.join(fields, ', ') + ' from account where industry = :ind limit 50'; // preferred: explicit bind map + access level map binds = new map { 'ind' => userinput }; list rows = database.querywithbinds(soql, binds, accesslevel.user_mode); // if you must concatenate user input, escape it string safe = string.escapesinglequotes(userinput); list r2 = database.query('select id from account where name like \\'%' + safe + '%\\''); // validate object/field names against describe before using them boolean ok = schema.getglobaldescribe().containskey(objectname.tolowercase()); integer total = database.countquery('select count() from account'); soql injection is the most common security review failure. never concatenate raw user input into a query string. use binds; whitelist object and field names."},{"id":"sosl","h":"SOSL","t":"find {acme*} in name fields returning account(id, name, industry where industry != null order by name limit 10), contact(id, name, email), lead(id, name, company) limit 50 part options find {term} wildcards * (any chars) and ? (one char); operators and , or , and not ; quoted phrases {\"big deal\"} in … fields all fields (default), name fields , email fields , phone fields , sidebar fields returning objects with fields, where, order by, limit per object with with division , with snippet (knowledge), with network string term = 'acme'; list > results = [find :term in all fields returning account(id, name), contact(id, name, email)]; list accounts = (list ) results[0]; list contacts = (list ) results[1]; // dynamic list > r = search.query('find \\'' + string.escapesinglequotes(term) + '*\\' returning lead(id)'); use soql when… use sosl when… you know the object and field you don't know which object or field holds the text you need exact filters, relationships, aggregates you need word/phrase matching across text, email, phone fields results must be complete relevance-ranked results are fine (search index is updated asynchronously, so very new records may not appear)"},{"id":"useful-queries-for-admins-and-developers","h":"Useful queries for admins and developers","t":"-- users with a permission set select assignee.name, assignee.username from permissionsetassignment where permissionset.name = 'sales_rep' -- members of a queue or public group select userorgroupid from groupmember where group.developername = 'support_tier_2' -- active flows and their versions (tooling api) select definition.developername, versionnumber, status from flow where status = 'active' -- apex classes by size (tooling api) select name, lengthwithoutcomments, apiversion from apexclass order by lengthwithoutcomments desc -- recent logins select userid, logintime, status, sourceip, application from loginhistory order by logintime desc limit 50 -- field changes on an opportunity select field, oldvalue, newvalue, createdby.name, createddate from opportunityfieldhistory where opportunityid = '006xxxxxxxxxxxx'"}]},{"url":"testing.html","title":"Testing Apex","intro":"testing apex tests are required to deploy apex to production, but their real job is to prove behaviour and catch regressions. good tests are bulk, assert outcomes, and run as realistic users.","sections":[{"id":"the-rules","h":"The rules","t":"at least 75% of apex lines across the org must be covered, every trigger must have some coverage, and all tests must pass to deploy to production. tests run against an empty database : they can't see org data (except setup objects like users, profiles, recordtypes) unless annotated @istest(seealldata=true) — avoid it. all data created in a test is rolled back at the end. no emails are actually sent; callouts must be mocked. test classes and methods are annotated @istest and don't count toward the org's apex size limit."},{"id":"anatomy-of-a-test-class","h":"Anatomy of a test class","t":"@istest private class opportunityservicetest { @testsetup static void makedata() { // runs once; each test method gets a fresh copy of this data account acc = testdatafactory.account('test co', true); testdatafactory.opportunities(acc.id, 200, true); } @istest static void wonopportunitiesgetonboardingtasks() { list opps = [select id from opportunity]; for (opportunity o : opps) o.stagename = 'closed won'; test.starttest(); // fresh governor limits from here update opps; test.stoptest(); // async work queued above runs now integer tasks = [select count() from task where subject = 'kick-off call']; assert.areequal(200, tasks, 'each won opportunity should get one task'); } @istest static void notaskswhenstageunchanged() { list opps = [select id, name from opportunity]; for (opportunity o : opps) o.name += ' (edited)'; update opps; assert.areequal(0, [select count() from task], 'no tasks for unrelated edits'); } @istest static void cannotclosewithoutproducts() { opportunity o = [select id from opportunity limit 1]; o.stagename = 'closed won'; try { update o; assert.fail('expected a dmlexception'); } catch (dmlexception e) { assert.istrue(e.getmessage().contains('add at least one product'), e.getmessage()); } } }"},{"id":"assertions","h":"Assertions","t":"method checks assert.areequal(expected, actual, msg) equality assert.arenotequal(a, b, msg) inequality assert.istrue(cond, msg) / isfalse booleans assert.isnull(v, msg) / isnotnull null checks assert.isinstanceoftype(obj, type, msg) type assert.fail(msg) fails immediately (e.g. after code that should have thrown) the older system.assertequals family still works. always pass a message: it's what you'll read when a deployment fails at 11pm."},{"id":"test-data-factory","h":"Test data factory","t":"@istest public class testdatafactory { public static account account(string name, boolean doinsert) { account a = new account(name = name, industry = 'technology', billingcountry = 'us'); if (doinsert) insert a; return a; } public static list opportunities(id accountid, integer n, boolean doinsert) { list opps = new list (); for (integer i = 0; i keep required fields and validation-rule-friendly values in one place, so a new validation rule means one fix, not fifty."},{"id":"running-as-another-user","h":"Running as another user","t":"@istest static void salesrepcannotseeothersprivateopps() { user rep = testdatafactory.user('sales_rep'); system.runas(rep) { // record sharing is enforced for the runas user in with-sharing code list visible = opportunityselector.myopenopportunities(); assert.areequal(0, visible.size(), 'rep should not see opportunities owned by others'); } } system.runas enforces record sharing and lets you test as a specific user, but doesn't enforce object/field permissions on its own — code using with user_mode or as user does. it also starts a new context, so you can insert setup objects (user, permissionsetassignment) and regular records in the same test without mixed_dml_operation ."},{"id":"testing-async-apex","h":"Testing async Apex","t":"async jobs enqueued between test.starttest() and test.stoptest() run synchronously at stoptest() . @istest static void batchclosesstaleleads() { list leads = new list (); for (integer i = 0; i"},{"id":"mocking-http-callouts","h":"Mocking HTTP callouts","t":"@istest public class erpmock implements httpcalloutmock { private final integer status; public erpmock(integer status) { this.status = status; } public httpresponse respond(httprequest req) { assert.istrue(req.getendpoint().startswith('callout:erp/'), 'uses the named credential'); httpresponse res = new httpresponse(); res.setstatuscode(status); res.setheader('content-type', 'application/json'); res.setbody('{\"ok\":' + (status { a.id })); test.stoptest(); assert.areequal('synced', [select erp_sync_status__c from account where id = :a.id].erp_sync_status__c); } for several endpoints in one test, write a mock that switches on req.getendpoint() , or use multistaticresourcecalloutmock with json files stored as static resources."},{"id":"testing-platform-events-and-cdc","h":"Testing platform events and CDC","t":"test.starttest(); eventbus.publish(new order_shipped__e(order_number__c = 'so-1')); test.stoptest(); // delivers the event; subscriber triggers run // or, mid-test: test.geteventbus().deliver(); // change data capture test.enablechangedatacapture(); insert new account(name = 'cdc test'); test.geteventbus().deliver(); // accountchangeevent triggers run"},{"id":"mocking-dependencies-with-the-stub-api","h":"Mocking dependencies with the Stub API","t":"// production class depends on an interface or a virtual class public virtual class pricingclient { public virtual decimal pricefor(string sku) { return null; } } @istest public class pricingstub implements system.stubprovider { public object handlemethodcall(object stubbed, string method, type returntype, list paramtypes, list paramnames, list args) { if (method == 'pricefor') return 42.0; return null; } } pricingclient mock = (pricingclient) test.createstub(pricingclient.class, new pricingstub()); quoteservice svc = new quoteservice(mock); // dependency injection via constructor assert.areequal(42.0, svc.unitprice('sku-1')); frameworks like apexmocks (part of fflib) build on the stub api with mockito-style syntax."},{"id":"other-test-helpers","h":"Other Test helpers","t":"method use test.isrunningtest() branch in production code (use sparingly) test.getstandardpricebookid() the standard price book in tests test.setcreateddate(id, datetime) backdate createddate test.setfixedsearchresults(ids) control sosl results test.loaddata(account.sobjecttype, 'staticresourcename') load records from a csv static resource @testvisible let tests access private members test.setcurrentpage(page) , restcontext.request = req set visualforce or rest context"},{"id":"running-tests","h":"Running tests","t":"# all local tests with coverage, human-readable sf apex run test --test-level runlocaltests --code-coverage --result-format human --wait 20 # specific classes or methods sf apex run test --class-names opportunityservicetest --tests opportunityservicetest.cannotclosewithoutproducts --wait 10 # get results of an earlier run sf apex get test --test-run-id 707xxxxxxxxxxxx --code-coverage in the developer console: test → new run; coverage shows per class (blue = covered, red = not). in vs code, click \"run test\" above a method."}]},{"url":"triggers.html","title":"Triggers","intro":"triggers a trigger is apex that runs before or after records are inserted, updated, deleted or undeleted. triggers always receive records in batches of up to 200, so every trigger must be written for a list.","sections":[{"id":"events-and-context","h":"Events and context","t":"event use for can change trigger.new ? before insert default and validate fields on new records yes — no dml needed before update derive fields, validate changes yes before delete block deletes ( adderror ) — after insert create related records (needs the new id) no (read-only) after update update related records, publish events, enqueue jobs no after delete clean up related data, recalculate parents — after undelete restore related data no variable contents available in trigger.new list of new record versions insert, update, undelete trigger.old list of old versions update, delete trigger.newmap id → new record before update, after insert/update/undelete trigger.oldmap id → old record update, delete trigger.operationtype system.triggeroperation enum: before_insert , after_update … all trigger.isbefore , isafter , isinsert , isupdate , isdelete , isundelete booleans all trigger.size number of records in this chunk all trigger.isexecuting true if the current code was called from a trigger any apex no newmap in before insert — the records don't have ids yet."},{"id":"the-handler-pattern","h":"The handler pattern","t":"keep exactly one trigger per object , with no logic in it. it delegates to a handler class, which keeps order predictable and code testable. trigger opportunitytrigger on opportunity ( before insert, before update, before delete, after insert, after update, after delete, after undelete ) { new opportunitytriggerhandler().run(); } public virtual class triggerhandler { // bypass by handler name, e.g. during data loads or from other code private static set bypassed = new set (); public static void bypass(string handlername) { bypassed.add(handlername); } public static void clearbypass(string handlername) { bypassed.remove(handlername); } public void run() { string name = string.valueof(this).substringbefore(':'); if (bypassed.contains(name) || featuremanagement.checkpermission('bypass_triggers')) return; switch on trigger.operationtype { when before_insert { beforeinsert(trigger.new); } when before_update { beforeupdate(trigger.new, trigger.oldmap); } when before_delete { beforedelete(trigger.old); } when after_insert { afterinsert(trigger.new); } when after_update { afterupdate(trigger.new, trigger.oldmap); } when after_delete { afterdelete(trigger.old); } when after_undelete { afterundelete(trigger.new); } } } protected virtual void beforeinsert(list newlist) {} protected virtual void beforeupdate(list newlist, map oldmap) {} protected virtual void beforedelete(list oldlist) {} protected virtual void afterinsert(list newlist) {} protected virtual void afterupdate(list newlist, map oldmap) {} protected virtual void afterdelete(list oldlist) {} protected virtual void afterundelete(list newlist) {} } public with sharing class opportunitytriggerhandler extends triggerhandler { protected override void beforeinsert(list newlist) { opportunityservice.defaultclosedate((list ) newlist); } protected override void beforeupdate(list newlist, map oldmap) { opportunityservice.requirelossreason((list ) newlist, (map ) oldmap); } protected override void afterupdate(list newlist, map oldmap) { list justwon = new list (); for (opportunity o : (list ) newlist) { opportunity old = (opportunity) oldmap.get(o.id); if (o.iswon && !old.iswon) justwon.add(o); } if (!justwon.isempty()) opportunityservice.createonboardingtasks(justwon); } } popular open-source frameworks (kevin o'hara's sfdc-trigger-framework , the fflib apex common domain layer, the trigger actions framework driven by custom metadata) follow the same idea with more features. pick one per org and use it everywhere."},{"id":"only-act-on-real-changes","h":"Only act on real changes","t":"update triggers fire on every save, even when the fields you care about didn't change. compare against oldmap : list changed = new list (); for (account a : (list ) trigger.new) { account old = (account) trigger.oldmap.get(a.id); if (a.billingcountry != old.billingcountry || a.ownerid != old.ownerid) { changed.add(a); } }"},{"id":"recursion-control","h":"Recursion control","t":"a trigger that updates records can cause itself (or another trigger that updates back) to fire again. options, from simplest: change detection (above): the second run sees no change and does nothing. the most robust option. a static set of processed ids : skip records already handled in this transaction. avoid a plain static boolean \"hasrun\" flag: with batches over 200 records, the second chunk of 200 would be skipped. public class accountsync { private static set processed = new set (); public static void syncchanged(list accounts) { list todo = new list (); for (account a : accounts) { if (processed.add(a.id)) todo.add(a); // add() returns false if already present } if (todo.isempty()) return; // ... work on todo ... } }"},{"id":"worked-examples","h":"Worked examples","t":"1. before insert/update: derive a field from the parent public static void setregion(list contacts, map oldmap) { set accountids = new set (); list todo = new list (); for (contact c : contacts) { boolean changed = oldmap == null || c.accountid != oldmap.get(c.id).accountid; if (c.accountid != null && changed) { accountids.add(c.accountid); todo.add(c); } } if (todo.isempty()) return; map accounts = new map ( [select id, region__c from account where id in :accountids]); for (contact c : todo) c.region__c = accounts.get(c.accountid)?.region__c; // before trigger: no dml — the changes are saved with the record } 2. before update: validation that needs a query public static void requireproducts(list opps, map oldmap) { set closing = new set (); for (opportunity o : opps) { if (o.iswon && !oldmap.get(o.id).iswon) closing.add(o.id); } if (closing.isempty()) return; set withproducts = new set (); for (aggregateresult ar : [select opportunityid from opportunitylineitem where opportunityid in :closing group by opportunityid]) { withproducts.add((id) ar.get('opportunityid')); } for (opportunity o : opps) { if (closing.contains(o.id) && !withproducts.contains(o.id)) { o.adderror('add at least one product before closing this opportunity as won.'); } } } 3. after insert: create related records public static void createwelcomecases(list accounts) { list cases = new list (); for (account a : accounts) { if (a.type == 'customer') { cases.add(new case(accountid = a.id, subject = 'welcome ' + a.name, origin = 'internal', priority = 'medium')); } } if (!cases.isempty()) insert cases; // one dml for the whole batch } 4. after insert/update/delete/undelete: roll up over a lookup public static void recountopencases(list newlist, list oldlist) { set accountids = new set (); for (case c : newlist ?? new list ()) if (c.accountid != null) accountids.add(c.accountid); for (case c : oldlist ?? new list ()) if (c.accountid != null) accountids.add(c.accountid); if (accountids.isempty()) return; map updates = new map (); for (id accid : accountids) updates.put(accid, new account(id = accid, open_cases__c = 0)); for (aggregateresult ar : [select accountid, count(id) n from case where accountid in :accountids and isclosed = false group by accountid]) { updates.get((id) ar.get('accountid')).open_cases__c = (integer) ar.get('n'); } update updates.values(); } // call from after insert, after update (pass trigger.old too, for re-parenting), // after delete (trigger.old) and after undelete (trigger.new). 5. before delete: prevent deletes public static void blockdeletewithinvoices(list oldlist) { set ids = new map (oldlist).keyset(); set withinvoices = new set (); for (invoice__c inv : [select account__c from invoice__c where account__c in :ids]) { withinvoices.add(inv.account__c); } for (account a : oldlist) { if (withinvoices.contains(a.id)) a.adderror('this account has invoices and can\\'t be deleted.'); } } 6. after update: hand off to async work public static void queueerpsync(list accounts, map oldmap) { set changed = new set (); for (account a : accounts) { account old = oldmap.get(a.id); if (a.name != old.name || a.billingstreet != old.billingstreet) changed.add(a.id); } if (!changed.isempty() && !system.isbatch() && !system.isfuture()) { system.enqueuejob(new erpaccountsyncjob(changed)); // callouts happen after commit } }"},{"id":"bypass-switches","h":"Bypass switches","t":"data migrations and integrations often need automation off. make it switchable without deployments: custom permission bypass_triggers assigned by permission set to the migration user (checked in the base class above). hierarchy custom setting with a checkbox per object, so you can turn off one object's triggers for one user. custom metadata listing active handlers (the trigger actions framework approach). static bypass in code: triggerhandler.bypass('accounttriggerhandler'); before a dml you don't want re-processed."},{"id":"triggers-vs-flow-on-the-same-object","h":"Triggers vs Flow on the same object","t":"both run in the same transaction. before-save flows run before before-triggers; after-save flows run after after-triggers (see order of execution ). mixing them on one object makes behaviour harder to follow — many teams choose one tool per object, or keep complex objects in apex and simple ones in flow."}]}];
